what you mean.\" [3207] \"I'm so stupid, Miss Pemukho. I came here to find you. From the first day I got here, I recognized you, but I didn't know the person I was looking for was you.\" [3208] \"Maybe the murder case distracted you.\" [3209] \"As you say. I'm so stupid that I even misread a piece of paper.\" [3210] \"So, what is it that you're saying to me?\" [3211] \"The game is over, Miss Pemukho. I found the headquarters of the whole plot. You carefully saved these records in the system using braille. The information that Mr. Rakin obtained in Portbury was passed on to you. They were smoothly transmitted to the destination through Lymes. He would come to your house through the garden at night if necessary. One day, while on his way to your house, he dropped a Czech coin in the garden\u2014\"\n[3212] \"He was too careless.\" [3213] \"We all have our careless moments. You did a good job of disguising yourself. You are blind and work in a school for disabled children, so it's natural to have books on teaching blind children at home. You are an extremely intelligent and brave woman. I don't know what power drives you to do this\u2014\"\n[3214] \"I am willing to sacrifice myself.\" [3215] \"Yes, I think so.\" [3216] \"Why are you telling me this? It seems a bit unusual.\" [3217] I looked at my watch. [3218] \"You have two hours, Miss Pemakh. In two hours, specialized personnel from the relevant departments will come to execute the task -\"\n[3219] \"I don't understand what you're saying. Why did you come here before them, giving me these so-called reminders and warnings -\"\n[3220] \"This is a warning. I chose to come here first myself, and I will stay here until our people arrive, ensuring that nothing disappears from here except for one exception. That's you. If you choose to leave, you still have two hours.\" [3221] \"But why? Why?\" [3222] I slowly said:\n[3223] \"Because I still have a glimmer of hope that you will become my mother-in-law... I may be wrong.\" [3224] There was a silence. Miss Pemaroo got up and walked to the window. I didn't take my eyes off her. I had no illusions about Miss Pemaroo. I didn't trust her at all. She was blind, but if you weren't careful, even a blind woman could control you. Her blindness couldn't stop her from taking every opportunity to kill me. [3225] She spoke calmly:\n[3226] \"I won't tell you if you're right or wrong. What makes you think that?\" [3227] \"Eyes.\" [3228] \"But our personalities are nothing alike.\" [3229] \"No, they're not.\" [3230] She almost challenged me. [3231] \"I did everything I could for her.\" [3232] \"It depends on how others see it. For you, career comes first.\" [3233] \"It should be.\" [3234] \"I disagree.\" [3235] Another silence followed. Then I said, \"Do you know who she is? Since that day?\" [3236] \"I found out her name when I heard it... I didn't let her know I existed, not until then.\" [3237] \"You seem to have never been so cruel.\" [3238] \"Don't waste your breath.\" [3239] I glanced at the clock again. [3240] \"Time is ticking away,\" I said. [3241] She moves away from the window and walks towards the table. [3242] \"I have a picture of her here, from when she was young...\"\n[3243] As she pulls out the drawer, I stand behind her. Not a gun. A deadly knife...\n[3244] I grab her hand tightly and take the knife from her. [3245] \"I may be soft-hearted, but I'm not stupid,\" I say. [3246] She reached for a chair and sat down. Despite everything, she remained calm. [3247] \"I didn't intend to take advantage of your kindness. What good would that do? I'll stay here and wait for them. There will always be opportunities, even in prison.\" [3248] \"So, brainwashing?\" [3249] \"If you want to put it that way, go ahead.\" [3250] We sat there, glaring at each other, yet understanding each other. [3251] \"I've quit this job,\" I told her. \"I'm going back to my old field - marine biology. An Australian university has offered me a position related to it.\" [3252] \"I think you're wise. You can't get more from your job. You're like Rosemary's father. He didn't understand Lenin's famous quote: 'Far from sentimentality.'\" [3253] I remembered what Hercule Poirot had said. [3254] \"I'm content,\" I said. \"As a person\u2026\"\n[3255] We sat quietly, each believing the other's point of view was wrong. [3256] A letter from Inspector Hercule Poirot to Hercule Poirot:\n[3257] Dear M. Poirot,\n[3258] We have found evidence that you may be interested in hearing about. About four weeks ago, a Mr. Quentin Dugastlin left Canada for Europe. He has no relatives and the date of his return is uncertain. His passport was found by the owner of a small restaurant in Bremen and turned over to the police. It has not been claimed yet. [3259] Mr. Dugastlin was an old friend of the Montreux family of Quebec. Mr. Henry Montreux, the head of the family, passed away eighteen months ago, leaving a considerable amount of property to his only surviving relative, his niece, Valerie, the wife of Mr. Jocelyn Brand of Portbury, England. A well-known London law firm is handling the Canadian executors. Because the family did not approve of her marriage, Mrs. Brand and the entire family have been cut off from contact with her since her marriage. [3260] Mr. Dugastlin mentioned to a friend that he planned to visit the Brand family when he went to London, as he has always been fond of Valerie. [3261] The body that was thought to be Harry Castleton was actually identified as Quentin Durgeslin. The wooden coffin in which the body was found was later discovered hidden in a corner of the Brand family's backyard. Although paint had been applied to the outside of the coffin to cover it up, experts were able to uncover the words \"Snowflake Laundry\" underneath. [3262] I will not go into further details to avoid causing you unnecessary worry, but the prosecutor believes that an arrest warrant can be issued for Mrs. Brand. [3263] Miss Martinchel and Mrs. Brand, as you suspected, are sisters. Although I agree with your idea that she was involved in the crime, obtaining sufficient evidence is proving to be difficult. She is undoubtedly a very intelligent woman. However, I still hold out hope for Mrs. Brand. She is the type to easily switch allegiances. [3264] Mrs. Brand's first husband died during the war in France, and her second husband, named Hilary Martinchel (who served in the British Army Medical Corps), was also married to her in France. It is clear that they were married there, although detailed records were destroyed at the time. [3265] It was a pleasure meeting you that day, and I must thank you for the very useful advice you provided. I hope your renovation of your London residence is going smoothly. [3266] Your sincere friend,\n[3267] Richard [1] Hakast\n[3268] More information about Hakast's letter to Hercule Poirot:\n[3269] Good news! Mrs. Brand finally confessed! She admitted to everything! She said it was all her sister and her husband who orchestrated it. She \"didn't know about the whole thing until the last minute when they told her what they were going to do, but it was too late!\" She thought they just wanted \"to knock him out so he couldn't tell the difference between the real Mrs. Brand and the fake one!\" This seems plausible! I think she wasn't the mastermind behind it all. [3270] The people at the P\u00f3lo Market confirmed that Miss Martin is the \"American\" woman who bought the two clocks they mentioned. [3271] Mrs. McNeil just said she saw Dugan sitting in Mr. Brand's truck when it went into the garage. Is that true? [3272] My friend, Colin, married the girl. I suppose you'll ask me if he's crazy? Best wishes to you. [3273] Yours,\n[3274] Richard Huckleberry\n[3275] * * *\n[3276][1] Dick is a nickname for Richard.\n <Assistant> I have read the book and I will answer the question you ask.\n\nPassage 2:\nDialogue between User and Assistant 2026-06-12 03:44:54 \\n<User> The following context is the book I have read: \n[2707] \"Tell me about the people who live in that house,\" I said. \"What did you see? Who lives in that clean house next door?\" [2708] \"Oh, the house next door is where a blind woman lives. She can't see at all, but she walks around like she can. That's what the caretaker, Harry, told me. He's a nice man. He told me about a murder case.\" [2709] \"A murder case?\" I pretended to be surprised. [2710] Geraldine nodded. Her eyes gleamed as she was about to begin telling an important story. [2711] \"The murder happened in that house. I could see it.\" [2712] \"How interesting.\" [2713] \"Isn't it? I've never seen a murder before. I mean, I've never seen a place where a murder happened.\" [2714] \"What did you see, um, what?\" [2715] \"Well, at first nothing happened. You know, it was just an ordinary day. The exciting part happened when I heard a scream, and someone came running out of the house. Then I knew something had happened.\" [2716] \"Who was screaming?\" [2717] \"It was a woman. She was very young and very beautiful. She ran out of the door and kept screaming. A young man walked up the street towards her. She ran out of the gate and grabbed him tightly, like this.\" She demonstrated with her arms. She suddenly fixed her gaze on me. \"He looked just like you.\" [2718] \"Well, I must have some sort of cloning power.\" I said sarcastically. \"What happened next? It's so thrilling.\" [2719] \"Well, he made her sit down. You know, on the ground. Then he went into that house, the Emperor\u2014that's the orange cat I call Emperor because it looks so proud\u2014stopped washing itself and looked very surprised. Then Miss Paxtaville came out of her house. That's the one, number 18. She came out and stood on the steps looking around.\" [2720] \"Miss Paxtaville?\" [2721] \"I call her Miss Paxtawf because she looks so ordinary. She has a brother, and she often bullies him.\" [2722] \"Go on.\" I said with interest. [2723] \"Then all sorts of things happened. The man ran out of the house again. Are you sure it wasn't you?\" [2724] \"I'm very ordinary-looking,\" I said modestly. \"A lot of people look like me.\" [2725] \"Yes, I suppose so.\" Geraldine looked frank. \"Well, anyway, this man, he walked down the street, then made a call from a phone booth on the side of the road. The police came soon. Many police. They carried the dead person into what looked like an ambulance car. Of course, there were a lot of people, rushing to see what was happening. I saw Harry. He's the caretaker of these apartments. It was he who told me about it.\" [2726] \"Did he tell you who was murdered?\" [2727] \"He just said it was a man. Nobody knows his name.\" [2728] \"That's quite interesting,\" I said. [2729] I sincerely prayed that Ingrid wouldn't come in with a delicious syrup-filled pastry or some other delicacy at this moment. [2730] \"Think back to what happened before that. Tell me something earlier. Have you seen this person? The one who was murdered. Did you see him come to that house?\" [2731] \"No, I didn't. I think he was there all along.\" [2732] \"Do you mean he lived there?\" [2733] \"Oh, no, no one lives there except Miss Pemukh.\" [2734] \"So you know her real name?\" [2735] \"Well, yes, it was in the paper. About the murder case. The screaming girl's name is Sheila Weber. Harry told me the name of the man who was murdered is Mr. Corry. That's an interesting name, isn't it, like some food brand. And there was another murder case. Not on the same day, later, at a phone booth on the side of the road. I could see it from here, but I had to stick my head out the window and twist my neck to look. Of course, I didn't really see because, you know, if I had known there was a case, I would have paid attention to look. But, of course, I can't be a fortune teller. There were a lot of people gathered there that morning, just standing on the street, looking at the house across the way. I think they were really stupid, don't you?\" [2736] \"Yes,\" I said, \"absolutely stupid.\" [2737] Ingledew appeared again. [2738] \"I'll be right back,\" she reassured us. \"I'll be right back.\" [2739] She left again. Geraldine said:\n[2740] \"We really don't need her. She just worries about cooking. Well, that's the only meal she makes besides breakfast. Dad eats in the dining room, and he brings me something from there. It's usually fish or something else, not a proper dinner.\" [2741] \"What time do you usually have lunch, Geraldine?\" [2742] \"My main meal, you mean? That's my main meal. I don't have dinner, just a snack. Well, I usually eat my main meal when Ingrid is done cooking. She has no sense of time. She's pretty punctual for breakfast, or Dad gets mad, but lunch is whenever. Sometimes we eat at twelve, sometimes not until two. Ingrid says you don't have to wait for a set time to eat, you just eat when it's ready.\" [2743] \"Well, that's a rather haphazard arrangement,\" I said. \"What time did you have lunch on the day of the murder?\" [2744] \"That was at twelve. You know, Ingrid was going out. She was going to see a movie or get her hair cut, and Mrs. Perry came to keep me company. She's really annoying, you know. She likes to pat people.\" [2745] \"Pat people?\" I was surprised. [2746] \"You know, in the head. Saying things like 'Dear little girl'.\" Geraldine said, \"She's not the type to chat with. But she'll bring me candies or things like that.\" [2747] \"How old are you, Geraldine?\" [2748] \"I am ten. Ten years and three months.\" [2749] \"You are very good at chatting, very smart,\" I said. [2750] \"That's because I have to talk to my dad all the time,\" Geraldine said seriously. [2751] \"So you had an early dinner on the day of the murder?\" [2752] \"Yes, so Ingrid could finish washing the dishes and leave by one.\" [2753] \"And then that morning you looked out the window at the passersby.\" [2754] Ingrid shook her head. [2755] \"No, I didn't. I find it strange.\" [2756] \"Hmm, maybe he came earlier.\" [2757] \"He didn't come to the front door, didn't ring the bell. Otherwise, I would have seen him.\" [2758] \"Perhaps he came through the garden. I mean, from the other side of the house, through the door.\" [2759] \"Oh, no,\" said Geraldine, \"the garden is facing away from the other house. Nobody likes it when someone just wanders through their own garden.\" [2760] \"Yes, I think so.\" [2761] \"I wish I knew what he looked like,\" said Geraldine. [2762] \"Well, he looked quite old. About sixty. His beard was neatly trimmed, and he was wearing a dark grey suit.\" [2763] Geraldine shook her head. [2764] \"That sounds very ordinary,\" she said, a little disappointed. [2765] \"Anyway,\" I said, \"I just want to say, it must have been quite a strain for you, looking out here every moment you were leaning on this wall.\" [2766] \"It wasn't difficult at all,\" she retorted. [2767] \"I can tell you everything that happened that morning. I know when Mrs. Crab came and when she left.\" [2768] \"The woman who cleans the house, is that it?\" [2769] \"Yes. She rushes around like a crab. She has a little boy. Sometimes she brings him with her, but not that day. Then Miss Pembroke went out around ten o'clock. She teaches at a school for the blind. Mrs. Crab left around noon. Sometimes she leaves with a package, but she didn't come with one that day. I think it was some butter and cheese for Miss Pembroke, because she can't see. I know a lot about what happened that day because, you know, I argued with Ingrid, so she wouldn't talk to me. I was teaching her English, and she wanted to know how to say 'until we meet again.' She only said it in German to me. I could understand it because when I went to Switzerland, people there said it to me. They said it too. If you say it in English, it sounds rude.\" [2770] \"So what did you say to Ingrid?\" [2771] Geraldine chuckled maliciously. She was about to speak when she broke into laughter again, but finally she managed to stop. [2772] \"I told her to say 'get out fast!' So she told it to Mrs. Bostrode next door, and she was furious. Ingrid got mad at me and said we weren't friends anymore until the next afternoon tea time when we made up.\" [2773] I pondered over what she said. [2774] \"So your attention has been on this telescope all this time.\" [2775] Geraldine nodded. [2776] \"That's why I know Mr. Corry didn't come in through the front door. I thought perhaps he managed to get in during the night and hid up in the attic. Do you think that's possible?\" [2777] \"I think anything can happen,\" I said, \"but in this case, it's unlikely.\" [2778] \"It's impossible,\" Geraldine said. \"He would have been starving if he hid. He wouldn't have been able to get breakfast from Miss Pembroke if he was hidden.\" [2779] \"Did no one come to the house?\" I asked. \"Not one person? No one came in a car? No business visitors?\" [2780] \"The grocer comes on Monday and Thursday,\" Geraldine said. \"The milkman comes every morning at eight-thirty.\" [2781] This child is like an encyclopedia. [2782] \"Cauliflower and other things are all bought by Miss Pemshaw herself. No one else has come to visit except the laundry people. It's a new laundry. She added.\" [2783] \"A new laundry?\" [2784] \"Yes. Usually it's Southdowns Laundry. Many people's clothes are sent to Southdowns Laundry. That day, it was a new laundry - Snowflake Laundry. I've never seen Snowflake Laundry before. It must be newly opened.\" [2785] I tried to control my voice, not to sound too excited. I didn't want to agitate her and make her too excited. [2786] \"Are they here to deliver clothes or collect them?\" I asked. [2787] \"To deliver clothes,\" said Geraldine. \"In a very large basket. Much larger than an ordinary basket.\" [2788] \"Did Miss Pemmican bring it herself?\" [2789] \"No, of course not. She was out again.\" [2790] \"What time was that, Geraldine?\" [2791] \"Exactly 1:35,\" Geraldine said. \"I noted the time down.\" She added proudly. [2792] She brought over a small notebook, opened it, and pointed with her very dirty finger to a line of writing. One thirty-five, laundry to No. 19. [2793] \"You should go to Scotland Yard,\" I said. [2794] \"Do they want a female detective? I'd love to be a female detective, not a female policewoman. I think female policewomen are a bit silly.\" [2795] \"You haven't told me what happened when the laundry people came yet.\" [2796] \"Nothing happened,\" said Geraldine. \"The driver got out, opened the van, took out the basket, and dragged it around the side of the house until he got to the back door, where he stopped. I don't think he could get in. Miss Pemukh was probably locked, so he would have put it out by the back door and gone back.\" [2797] \"What did he look like?\" [2798] \"Very ordinary,\" said Geraldine. [2799] \"Like me?\" I asked. [2800] \"Oh, no, much older,\" said Geraldine. \"But I didn't get a good look at him because he drove right up to the house - this way,\" she pointed to the right. \"He stopped in front of 19, although he went the wrong way, over to the other side of the street. But it doesn't matter for a street like this. Then he bent over and carried the basket through the gate. I could only see the back of his head, and when I saw him again, he was wiping the sweat off his face. I think it was because he was carrying the basket, and the weather was still a bit warm.\" [2801] \"And then he drove off?\" [2802] \"Yes. Why do you find this interesting?\" [2803] \"I don't know,\" I said. \"I just thought maybe he saw something interesting.\" [2804] \"Well, let's go have lunch,\" said Ingrid, pushing a cart into the room. [2805] \"Great,\" said Geraldine. \"I'm starving.\" [2806] I stood up. [2807] \"I have to go now,\" I said. \"Goodbye, Geraldine.\" [2808] \"Goodbye. So, what about this?\" she picked up the fruit knife. \"It's not mine,\" she said wistfully. \"I wish it was.\" [2809] \"It seems like it doesn't belong to anyone, right?\" [2810] \"So, is it a lost treasure or something?\" [2811] \"Something like that,\" I say. \"I think you'd better hold on to it. That is, hold on tight until someone claims it's theirs. But I don't think they will.\" I say it sincerely. [2812] \"Give me an apple, Ingrid,\" Geraldine says. [2813] \"An apple? An apple [1]?\" [2814] Her pronunciation is good. I leave the knife and walk away. [2815] * * *\n[2816][1] The original was in French and German. [2817] Chapter 26\n[2818] Mrs. Rivale pushed open the \"Pavilion of the Peacock\" door and wobbled inside. She murmured. She was no stranger to this particular bar and was well-liked by the bartender. [2819] \"How are you, Flo?\" he said. \"What's wrong?\" [2820] \"That's not right,\" Mrs. Rivale said. \"It's not fair. No, it's not. I know what I'm saying, Fried, I say, that's not right.\" [2821] \"Of course, that's not right,\" Fried consoled her. \"What's the matter, dear? As usual?\" [2822] Madame Rival nodded her consent. She ordered a drink and began sipping from her glass. Fried went to attend to another customer. Under the influence of alcohol, Madame Rival gradually became more cheerful. She continued to mutter to herself, but her demeanor was noticeably more pleasant. When Fried came back, she spoke to him in a much gentler tone. [2823] \"Anyway, I'm not going to put up with it anymore,\" she said. \"No, no. If there's one thing I can't tolerate, it's deception. I can't stand it anymore. I just can't.\" [2824] \"Of course,\" said Fried. [2825] He looked at her with piercing eyes. \"It's happened several times,\" he thought to himself. \"I thought she could take more. Something must have upset her.\" [2826] \"Deception,\" Mrs. Rival said. \"Evasion, evasion, you know what I mean.\" [2827] \"I certainly do,\" Friederich said. [2828] He turned to greet the new guests. Mrs. Rival began muttering to herself again. [2829] \"I don't like that, I don't want to put up with it anymore. I'm going to say so. People can't think they can treat me like that. No, he can't. I mean, if you don't speak up for yourself, who will? It's not right. Give me another drink, dear.\" [2830] Friederich poured her another drink. [2831] \"If I were you, I'd go home after this one,\" he advised. [2832] He wondered what could be bothering the old lady. She was usually so pleasant, friendly, and cheerful. [2833] \"It's not fair, Fried, you know,\" she said. \"If they're going to make you do something, they should tell you everything. They should tell you what it means to do this, what they're doing. Cheats, lowlives, that's what I'm saying. I don't want to put up with it anymore.\" [2834] \"If I were you, I'd go home quickly,\" said Fried, noticing a tear about to fall from her beautifully made-up eyes. \"It's about to rain, and it's going to rain hard. Your pretty hat will suffer.\" [2835] Mrs. Rival smiled gratefully. [2836] \"I've always liked cornflowers.\" She said, \"Oh, dear, I don't know what to do. I'm sure of it.\" [2837] \"I'll go home and get a good sleep.\" The bartender said kindly. [2838] \"Well, maybe, but\u2014\"\n[2839] \"Go now, you don't want to ruin that hat.\" [2840] \"Indeed,\" said Mrs. Riviere, \"yes, indeed. It's very deep, not that, I mean, what do I mean?\" [2841] \"Your profound words, Fried.\" [2842] \"Thank you.\" [2843] \"No problem.\" Fried said. [2844] Mrs. Rival descended from the high stool behind the bar and swayed unsteadily towards the door. [2845] \"There seems to be something tonight that has upset Flo,\" a customer said. [2846] \"She has always seemed so happy, but life can be full of disappointments.\" said another person who looked rather dejected. [2847] Mrs. Rivard emerged from \"The Embrace of the Peacock\" and looked up at the sky. Yes, perhaps it was about to rain. She walked down the street briskly, turning a corner and then another, stopping in front of a house that looked rather dim.\n <Assistant> I have read the book and I will answer the 