n her face brightened again. \"Maybe my husband will remember.\" [1130] \"Is he home?\" [1131] \"Oh, he's out in the garden,\" she said, pointing out the window. An older man was pushing a wheelbarrow along a path. [1132] \"Perhaps we should go out and talk to him.\" [1133] \"Of course, come this way.\" [1134] She led them through a side door and into the garden. Mr. MacNorton looked flushed and sweaty. [1135] \"These two gentlemen are from the police station, Angus,\" his wife panted. \"They want to ask about the murder at Miss Pemmican's house. Here's a picture of the deceased. Do you know, I'm sure I've seen him before. Was it last week when he came asking if we had any antiques to dispose of? Was it that man?\" [1136] \"Let me see,\" Mr. McNaughton said, \"just hold it up for me. I've got mud on my hands and can't touch anything.\" He addressed Mr. Harkness, \"You know about my gardening, don't you?\" [1137] \"Yes, Mr. Harkness said so,\" Mr. McNaughton grunted. [1138] \"Not Mrs. Lymes, I hope.\" [1139] \"No, it was Mr. Brand.\" [1140] Angus McNaughton snorted. [1141] \"Brand doesn't know what gardening is,\" he said, \"he just digs holes and then plants begonias, geraniums, and Cape tulips. That's not what I call gardening. Those flowers would survive in the park. Are you interested in shrubs, Inspector? Of course, it's not the season for planting shrubs, but I have a couple of shrubs here that you might be surprised to see I've kept alive. It's said that shrubs thrive best in Devonshire and Cornwall.\" [1142] \"I'm afraid I'm not much of a gardener,\" said Mr. Huckleberry. [1143] Mr. McNaughton looked at him as an artist might look at someone who doesn't understand art but knows what they like. [1144] \"Perhaps I should start talking about the unpleasant topic,\" said Mr. Huckleberry. [1145] \"Certainly. It happened yesterday, I was in the garden.\" [1146] \"Really?\" [1147] \"Well, I mean, when I heard that girl's scream, I was here.\" [1148] \"What were you doing?\" [1149] \"Well,\" Mr. MacNoughton said, startled, \"I didn't do anything. In fact, I thought it was those two noisy kids from the Lymeses' house. They're always yelling, screaming, making a racket.\" [1150] \"But the scream came from a different direction, didn't it?\" [1151] \"Yes, if those boys had stayed in their own garden. But they don't, you know. They're always jumping over other people's fences and hedges. They're chasing poor Mrs. Black's cats all over the place. Nobody's watching them, that's the trouble. Their mother's a nice, quiet sort. Of course, when there's no man in the house, the children get out of hand.\" [1152] \"I know Mr. Rimmers often goes abroad.\" [1153] \"He's an engineer, as far as I know,\" Mr. McNaughton said vaguely, \"always on the move. Dam building, or pipelines, or something like that. I'm not criticizing, dear, but I mean this kind of dam building, or laying oil pipelines, or whatever. I don't know the real story. He rushed off to Sweden a month ago, leaving a lot of work for the children's mother\u2014cooking and housework. Well, no wonder they're getting wild. They're not bad at heart, they just need discipline.\" [1154] \"Did you see anything besides hearing the scream? I mean, when did you hear it, by the way?\" [1155] \"I'm not sure. I usually take off my watch when I go into the garden. The other day, the water pipe was broken, and it took me a while to fix it. What time was that, dear? You heard it too, didn't you?\" [1156] \"Probably around half past two, at least half an hour after we had lunch.\" [1157] \"I see. When do you have lunch?\" [1158] \"At half past one,\" Mr. McNaughton said. \"If we're lucky. Our Danish girl doesn't have a good sense of time.\" [1159] \"And then what? Do you take a nap?\" [1160] \"Sometimes. But not today. I need to finish what I started. I cleared up a lot of waste and added it to the compost pile.\" [1161] \"Great, making compost,\" said Hakaste seriously. [1162] Mr. McNuton immediately brightened up. [1163] \"Absolutely right. Nothing beats homemade compost. I've convinced many people of that. Using chemical fertilizers is like self-destruction, isn't it? Let me show you.\" [1164] He enthusiastically pulled Hakaste's arm and pushed the wheelbarrow along the path to the fence separating his garden from No. 19. The compost was surrounded by lilacs, making it look enticing. Mr. McNuton pushed the wheelbarrow to a small shed next to it. The shed was neatly arranged with various tools. [1165] \"You've organized everything so well,\" commented Hakaste. [1166] \"You must take care of your tools,\" McNotton said. [1167] Hakaste closely observed the 19th garden. On the other side of the fence was a path lined with roses, leading to the corner of the house. [1168] \"Did you see anyone in the 19th garden or peering out the window while you were making compost?\" [1169] McNotton shook his head. [1170] \"I didn't see anyone. Sorry I couldn't help you, Detective.\" [1171] \"You know, Angus,\" his wife said, \"I'm sure I saw a shadowy figure hiding in the garden of number 19.\" [1172] \"I don't think you did, dear,\" her husband said firmly. \"I didn't either.\" [1173] \"That woman kept saying she saw this and that, as if she could spot anything,\" Hakaste grumbled as they got back in the car. [1174] \"Do you think she recognized the person in the photo?\" [1175] Hakaste shook his head. \"I doubt it. She's just imagining she's seen him. I know this kind of witness. When I asked her to clarify, she couldn't answer, right?\" [1176] \"That's right.\" [1177] \"Of course, she could have said she saw him on the bus or somewhere else, sitting across from her. But if you ask me, I think it's just her wishful thinking. What do you think?\" [1178] \"I agree.\" [1179] \"We didn't learn anything,\" Hakast sighed. \"Of course, some things seem strange. For example, Mrs. Black, no matter how she tried to associate herself with the cat, shouldn't be completely unaware of her neighbor Miss Pema, which seems impossible. And she was so confused about the murder case, showing no concern.\" [1180] \"She's just a muddled woman.\" [1181] \"No brains!\" Hakaste said. \"When you encounter such a silly woman, fires, thefts, and murders happening around them, they won't even pay attention.\" [1182] \"Her wire mesh fence is quite effective. The Victorian shrubs almost hide everything from view.\" [1183] They returned to the police station. Hakaste grinned at his friend:\n[1184] \"Well, Detective Blue, I can now let you go home.\" [1185] \"No more interviews?\" [1186] \"Not for now. I'll be visiting a few more places later, but not with you.\" [1187] \"All right, then. Thank you for this morning. Will you have my notes typed up? Here's the folder.\" \"You said the interrogation is tomorrow, isn't it? What time?\" [1188] \"Eleven o'clock.\" [1189] \"Good. I'll be back then.\" [1190] \"You're leaving?\" [1191] \"I'm going to London to give a report tomorrow. I can guess who it is.\" [1192] \"Don't say anything.\" [1193] Hakaste grinned. [1194] \"Say hello to that guy for me.\" [1195] \"Also, I might go see an expert,\" said Colin. [1196] \"An expert? What kind of expert? What's going on?\" [1197] \"Nothing, just stupid. Not that kind of expert. He's in your line of work. He's my father's friend, and he's my friend. You know, he loves these kind of things. It excites him. I think he needs a little stimulation.\" [1198] \"Scotland Yard?\" [1199] \"No. A private detective, a friend of my father's, and also a friend of mine. Your bizarre work would be right up his alley. He enjoys these things. It would excite him. I think he needs some stimulation.\" [1200] \"What's his name?\" [1201] \"Hercule Poirot.\" [1202] \"I've heard of him. I thought he was dead.\" [1203] \"He's still alive. But I feel he has nothing to do. It's worse than being dead.\" [1204] \"You're a strange guy, Colin. Having such strange friends. \" \"And you're no different,\" Colin said with a grin. [1205] ***\n[1206] [1] In English, the words \"dam\" (as in a dam) and \"damn\" are pronounced the same. [1207] Chapter 12\n[1208] After sending Colin away, Inspector Hercule looked at the neatly written address on his notepad and nodded. Then he quietly put the notebook back in his pocket and began to deal with the pile of daily files on his desk. [1209] He worked all day. He sent someone out to buy him coffee and sandwiches, received a report from Sergeant Grey - no useful leads were found. No one recognized Mr. Kuri's photo at the train station or bus station. The check of the clothing in the laboratory was also fruitless. The suit was made by a skilled tailor, but the label of the tailor's shop had been cut off. Was the information that Mr. Kuri wanted to hide his identity or the identity of the killer? The detailed report on the deceased's teeth had been distributed to various places, which was likely the most useful clue. It would take some time, but eventually, there would be a result. Unless, Mr. Kuri was a foreigner? Hercule imagined, it was likely that the deceased was French, but his clothes were not French-made, and there were no laundry shop labels to inquire about. [1210] Inspector Hercule was not impatient. Confirming the identity is often a slow process. But in the end, someone would come forward to confirm it, perhaps the laundry shop owner, the dentist, the doctor, or the landlady. Mr. Kuri's photo would be sent to various police stations and would be published in newspapers. Soon, Mr. Kuri's true identity would be discovered. [1211] Meanwhile, there was other work to be done, not just Mr. Kirby's case. Detective Hakast worked non-stop until 5:30 PM. He glanced at his watch again and thought it was a suitable time to visit a few more places. [1212] According to Inspector Gray's report, Ms. Sheila Webber had returned to Cavenish Typewriting Agency and had an appointment with Professor Puddy at the Heron Hotel at 5:00 PM, and she would not leave until after 6:00 PM. [1213] What was the name of the aunt? Mrs. Rotten, Mrs. Rotten. Living at 14 Palmerston Road. He did not drive his police car but chose to walk the shortcut. [1214] It was said that Palmerston Road used to be a lifeless road. Detective Hakast noticed that most of the houses had been converted into apartments or small houses. As he turned the corner, a girl was walking along the sidewalk towards him. She seemed to hesitate for a moment. Detective Hakast was thinking about other things at the time, and he thought she was going to ask him for directions. But if that was the case, why did she change her mind and continue to walk past him? He wondered why he suddenly thought of shoes. Shoes... No, just one shoe. This girl made him feel vaguely familiar. Who was she? Someone he had seen recently... maybe she recognized him and wanted to talk to him? [1215] He stopped and looked back at her. She was walking quickly by now. The problem was, her face had no clear and distinct features, making it hard to remember, unless there was a special reason. She had blue eyes, fair skin, and a slightly open mouth. The mouth. This made him think of something else. What was she doing with her mouth? Speaking? Putting on lipstick? No. He was a bit annoyed with himself. Detective Hakast prided himself on being able to remember people's facial features. He could say that he would never forget the people he had seen in the defendant's and witness's stands, but after all, he didn't just deal with those people. He couldn't remember, for example, every waitress who had served him. He couldn't remember every bus conductor. So he decided not to think about it for now. [1216] He arrives at number 14. The door is half open, and there are four bells beside it with names written underneath. He sees Mrs. Rudd. She lives in the ground-floor apartment. He goes in and rings the doorbell on the left of the hall. After a few minutes, he hears movement. Soon, footsteps are heard from inside. A tall, thin woman opens the door, her black hair disheveled, wearing a large apron, and seems to be out of breath. There is an onion smell coming from the house, obviously from the kitchen. [1217] \"Is this Mrs. Rudd?\" [1218] \"What do you want?\" She looks at him suspiciously and seems a little annoyed. [1219] He thinks she is about forty-five years old and looks a bit like a gypsy. [1220] \"What do you want?\" [1221] \"Could I trouble you for a moment to discuss something?\" [1222] \"Well, what is it? I'm very busy now.\" She spoke quickly, \"Are you a reporter?\" [1223] \"Of course not,\" Detective Hackett said sympathetically, \"I imagine you've been bothered by reporters quite a bit, haven't you?\" [1224] \"Yes, they keep knocking on the door and ringing the bell, asking all sorts of stupid questions.\" [1225] \"That can be quite irritating, I know,\" Detective Hackett said, \"I hope we can help alleviate some of that for you, Mrs. Rutter. I'm Detective Hackett, by the way, in charge of the case that has reporters bothering you. We'll do our best to prevent that from happening, but unfortunately, we can't control the press, you know, they have their own rights.\" [1226] \"It's inconsiderate of them to bother others like that,\" Mrs. Rudd said, \"claiming they have to report the news for the public. I've seen their reports in the newspaper, and they're all full of nonsense. As far as I know, they just make up facts. Please come in, Detective.\" [1227] She stepped back a few steps, and Detective Hakast walked through the doorway. She closed the door. There were several envelopes on the doormat. Mrs. Rudd was about to bend down to pick them up when Detective Hakast politely beat her to it. He glanced at the envelopes, checking the addresses, before handing them over to the hostess. [1228] \"Thank you.\" [1229] She placed them on the table in the hall. [1230] \"Go ahead to the living room, please? I'll be right there. The food in the kitchen is almost ready.\" [1231] She hurried off to the kitchen. Detective Hackett glanced one last time at the letters on the hall table. One was addressed to Mrs. Rotten, and the other two were for Miss R.S. Webber. He followed Mrs. Rotten's directions into the room. It was a small, messy room with old, stained furniture and unidentifiable stains and spots everywhere. There was an eye-catching, expensive Venetian cast glass dish, an abstract model, two colorful velvet cushions, and an exotic-looking large ceramic shallow dish. He thought that either the aunt or the niece had some taste. [1232] Mrs. Rotten returned, panting even more than before.\n <Assistant> I have read the book and I will answer the question you ask.\n\nPassage 4:\nDialogue between User and Assistant 2026-06-12 03:44:54 \\n<User> The following context is the book I have read: \nThere is an important statement.\" [3092] \"What?\" I asked. \"Who said it? When?\" [3093] \"At the appropriate time, dear.\" [3094] \"Please tell me, Monsieur Poirot,\" the inspector politely returned to the topic. [3095] \"If you draw a circle around number 19, all the people inside it could have killed Mr. Corry. Mrs. Hemm, the Brandt couple, the MacNodon couple, Miss Watrous. But most importantly, some people have already been set up in the scene. Miss Pemerton may have killed him before going out at about 1:35 p.m., and Miss Webb may have been arranged to meet him, killing him before running out of the room to call the police.\" [3096] \"Ah,\" said the detective, \"now you're getting to the specifics.\" [3097] \"Of course,\" said Poirot, sliding his wheelchair, \"you, my dear Colwyn. You were also on the scene. Finding the lower half of the house on the upper half of the street.\" [3098] \"Oh, really?\" I said indignantly, \"What do you mean?\" [3099] \"I mean that I know all there is to know!\" Poirot declared pompously. [3100] \"And I'm the one who's still trying to tell you the whole thing!\" [3101] \"Murderers are usually arrogant,\" Poirot pointed out, \"and may well play games with you so you have a reason to laugh at me.\" [3102] \"If you go on like this, you'll convince me,\" I said. [3103] I began to feel a little uneasy. [3104] Poirot turned to Inspector Hercule. [3105] \"I say to myself, essentially, it's a simple case of murder. The appearance of the clock, the time advanced by an hour, the deliberate discovery of the body, all of this must be put aside. The essential thing is that an ordinary middle-aged man has died and that someone wanted him to die. If we knew who the dead man was, it would suggest who the murderer is. If he was a notorious con artist, we would have to find those he may have cheated; if he was a detective, we would look for those who had committed a crime under his nose; if he was a rich man, we would look among his heirs. But if we don't know who the man was, finding a suspect from this vast pool is a very daunting task.\" [3106] \"Without considering Miss Pemukh and Miss Hila Webber, who should not have been there, the answer is disappointing. I think only Mr. Lymes has something strange?\" Poirot looked at me inquiringly, and I nodded. \"Everyone has a reason to be trusted. Brand is a famous local architect, MacNorton is a professor at Cambridge University, Mrs. Hamm is the widow of a local auctioneer, and the Waterhouse siblings are respected locals. So let's go back to Mr. Curry. Where does he come from? What brought him to 19 Wilbraham Crescent? The neighbor, Mrs. Hamm, said something very revealing when she found out that the dead man was not living at number 19. 'Oh! I see. He came here to die. How strange.' She has a talent for seeing the essence of things, a talent that only those who are only concerned with their own actions and do not care about others possess. She summed up the whole case. Mr. Curry came to 19 Wilbraham Crescent to die. That's all there is to it!\" [3107] \"Her words suddenly startled me,\" I said. [3108] Poirot did not notice me. [3109] \" 'Dear, dear, dear, come to die.' Mr. Curry came and was killed. But that's not all. His identity cannot be confirmed, which is important. He had no wallet, no identification, and the brand name on his clothes had been torn off. But that's not enough. The card of an insurance agent named Curry was just a temporary measure. If the man's identity cannot be confirmed, he will eventually be given a false identity. It could be his brother, his sister, or his wife. As for the wife, Mrs. Rivale, just the name may have already aroused suspicion. There is a village called Curry-Rivale in Somerset, where I and a friend live nearby \u2013 I don't know what the two names suggest, Mr. Curry, Mrs. Rivale.\" [3110] \"At this point, the plan is almost clear, but what confuses me is why our killer assumes that the police will never be able to determine the actual identity of the deceased. If the man had no family, at least there would be a butler, a servant, or a business partner. This leads me to another assumption: no one knows that the man has disappeared. Another assumption is that he is not British, but only a traveler in this country. This fits with the fact that he had traces of having his teeth treated, but there was no record of any treatment.\" [3111] \"My mind began to form a vague picture of the victim and the killer. The case was carefully planned and skillfully executed, but now there is a flaw that the killer did not anticipate.\" [3112] \"What?\" Hercule asked. [3113] To my surprise, at this point Poirot tilted his head back dramatically and recited a poem:\n[3114] A nail lost, a shoe lost,\n[3115] A shoe lost, a horse lost,\n[3116] One war horse was lost, one war was lost,\n[3117] One war was lost, one empire was lost,\n[3118] All because one horseshoe nail was missing. [3119] He leaned forward. [3120] \"There may be many suspects for the murder of Mr. Curry. But there is only one who could have killed, or had a reason to kill, the girl Iona.\" [3121] We all turned to him at the same time. [3122] \"Let's consider Cavenish Typewriting Agency. There are eight girls working there. On September 9th, four of them were out on an errand, so they wouldn't be back until after lunch. Normally, they would have lunch between 12:30 and 1:30 p.m. The other four, Hilary Webb, Ingrid Brent, and two others, would have lunch between 1:30 and 2:30 p.m. But on that day, Ingrid Brent had a small accident on her way back to the office. Her shoe heel got stuck in the grating, making it difficult for her to walk. So she bought some bread and went straight back to the office.\" [3123] Poirot wagged his strong finger at us. [3124] \"We know that Ingrid Brent was worried about something. She tried to meet Hilary Webb outside the office, but failed. Could this be related to Hilary Webb? We couldn't find any evidence. She might have just wanted to ask Hilary Webb about something that was bothering her. But there was one thing that was clear. She wanted to talk to Hilary Webb outside the office.\" [3125] \"Her statement to the police, that morning, was the only clue we had to what was bothering her. She said, 'I don't know if what she said is true.' Three women testified that day. Ingrid might have been referring to Miss Pemshaw. Or, as we had previously surmised, she might have been referring to Hilary Webb. But there was a third possibility. She might have been referring to Miss Martin.\" [3126] \"Miss Martin? But her testimony only lasted a few minutes.\" [3127] \"Indeed, she only stated the phone call she received claiming it was from Miss Pemerton.\" [3128] \"Are you saying that Ina knew that the phone call was not from Miss Pemerton?\" [3129] \"My inference is even simpler. I believe there was no such phone call at all.\" [3130] He continued speaking:\n[3131] \"Ina's heel broke off. The grille was close to the office. So she returned to the typing pool quickly. But Miss Martindeer was in her own office, unaware that Ina had returned. As far as she was concerned, there was only herself in the office at that time. All she had to do was say that a call came in at 1:49. Ina did not know the severity of the situation at first. Hill went to see Miss Martindeer, who was told there was an appointment to go out. The nature and time of the appointment, Ina did not know. [3132] \"Then the news of the murder broke, and the story unfolded little by little. Miss Pemarho called and asked Hill to come to her. But Miss Pemarho said she had not made the call. The call was said to have come at 2:50 p.m. But Ina knew it was not true. There was no call at that time. Miss Martindeer must have been mistaken, but Miss Martindeer was sure she was not mistaken. The more Ina thought about it, the more puzzled she became. She had to ask Hill. Hill might know. [3133] \"Then the interrogation began. All the girls attended. Miss Martindeer repeated her statement about the call, but Ina knew that the clear evidence and precise time she provided were false. So she went to ask the officer to speak to the chief inspector. I think it's likely that Miss Martindeer overheard her question as she left with the crowd. Or perhaps she heard the girls talking about Ina breaking her heel at that moment, realizing the truth. Anyway, she followed the girl to Wilbraham Crescent. I wonder why Ina went there? [3134] \"Just to see the scene of the murder, I guess,\" Hakaste sighed. [3135] \"Yes, that's right. Maybe Miss Martindeer was talking to her, and they were walking down the street together when Ina casually expressed her doubts. Miss Martindeer decided to take immediate action. They happened to be walking past a phone booth. She said, 'This is very important. You must call the police immediately. Tell them we are going to see them right now.' Following someone's instructions was a habit Ina had formed. She went inside, picked up the phone, and Miss Martindeer followed her, standing behind her, tightening her scarf around her neck, and choking her to death.\" [3136] \"Did nobody see?\" [3137] Poirot shrugged. [3138] \"It should have been someone who saw! But it was exactly 1:00. Lunchtime. And people on New Moon Street were busy looking at this and that. This gave the bold and shameless person the opportunity to act.\" [3139] Hercule Poirot shook his head, incredulous. [3140] \"Miss Martin? I don't understand how she got involved in this case?\" [3141] \"Indeed, it was not immediately apparent. But there is no doubt that Miss Marple killed Ingrid. Oh, yes. Only she could have killed Ingrid, so she must be involved. From Miss Marple, I began to suspect that this murder was a Macbethian[1] one, a cruel and unimaginative boring woman.\u201d\n[3142] \"Lacking imagination?\" Hercule Poirot asked. [3143] \"Oh, yes, very lacking. But very efficient. A first-rate conspirator.\u201d\n[3144] \"But the motive? What was her reason for doing it?\" [3145] Hercule Poirot looks at me. He wags a finger. [3146] \"So, your neighbors' gossip was of no use to you, was it? I found a statement that intrigued me. Do you remember when talking about living abroad, Mrs. Brand said she liked living in Crofton because she had an aunt there. But Mrs. Brand actually had no aunt. She inherited a large sum of money from her uncle in Canada, who was her only surviving relative in the family.\" [3147] Hercule Poirot sat up alertly. [3148] \"So you think \u2013\"\n[3149] Poirot leaned back in his chair, folding his fingers together. He half-closed his eyes, as if dreaming. [3150] \"For example, you are a man, a very ordinary, cautious man, with financial difficulties. One day, you receive a letter from a law firm saying that your wife has inherited a large sum of money from her uncle in Canada. The letter was addressed to Mrs. Brand, but the problem is that the Mrs. Brand who received the letter was not the one mentioned in the letter. She was the second wife, not the first. Imagine how sad and frustrating that would be! But suddenly, there's a solution. Who would know that this was not Mrs. Brand? In Crofton, no one knew that Mr. Brand had been married before. His first marriage was many years ago, during the war, when he was overseas. His wife died soon after they married, and he remarried quickly. He had the original marriage certificate, various other documents, and photos of all the relatives in Canada. Everything was in order. Anyway, it was worth taking a risk. They tried, and succeeded. They went through all the legal procedures. The Brand couple became rich, and their financial difficulties ended \u2013\"\n[3151] \"However, a year later, something unexpected happened to them. What happened? I assume that someone from Canada came to this country and knew all the details about the first Mrs. Brand, and the truth was about to come out. He could be an elderly family executor or a close friend of the family, but whoever he is, he would know. Perhaps they thought they could avoid meeting. Mrs. Brand could pretend to be sick, or she could go abroad, but any similar thing would arouse suspicion. The visitor would insist on seeing the woman he had come to visit specially.\" [3152] \"So, they decided to murder him?\" [3153] \"Yes. Here, I think Mrs. Brand's sister is the mastermind behind the plot.\" [3154] \"You mean Miss Martin and Mrs. Brand are sisters?\" [3155] \"Only then does the story make sense.\" [3156] \"When I saw Mrs. Brand, she reminded me of someone,\" said Hercule Poirot, \"although their personalities were very different, but upon careful reflection, they were somewhat alike. But how could they hope to escape? Someone is missing. They would face interrogation \u2013\"\n[3157] \"If this person went abroad, perhaps just for a vacation, not for business, his schedule would not be fixed. Receiving letters from one place, then postcards from another. By the time people start to suspect, a long time may have passed. Who would associate the already-identified Harry Castleton with the wealthy tourist from Canada who never even set foot in the country? If I were the killer, I would go to France or Belgium for a carefree day of sightseeing, and then deliberately throw the dead man's passport in a train or a car, so that the interrogation could take place there.\" [3158] I unconsciously moved, and Poirot stared at me intently. [3159] \"Right?\" he said. [3160] \"Brand told me he had gone to Bournemouth for a day trip with a blonde woman, and I thought \u2013\"\n[3161] \"That's perfectly normal. Don't doubt it, it's his old habit.\" [3162] \"But these are just conjectures,\" Hercule countered. [3163] \"But you can do your own investigation,\" Hercule said. [3164] He picked up a hotel stationery sheet from the shelf in front of him and handed it to Hercule. [3165] \"You can write to Mr. Endebie at 10 Innisfree Gardens, SW7, who has promised to investigate for me in Canada. He is a well-known international lawyer.\" [3166] \"What about those clocks then?\" [3167] \"Oh! Those clocks! Those remarkable clocks!\" Poirot laughed. \"I think you will find that Miss Rosemary will be held responsible for them. Hence this case, as I said, is quite simple, only made to look fantastic. That clock that Miss Hyde took to be repaired. Did she leave it at the typewriter repair shop? Miss Marple took advantage of this to base her crazy talk on it, perhaps because of that clock, she chose Miss Hyde to be the one who discovered the body\u2014?\" [3168] Hercule suddenly exclaimed loudly:\n[3169] \"You still say this woman is boring and lacks imagination? When did she start planning all this?\" [3170] \"But these were not her plans. That's what makes it interesting. Everything was here, waiting for her. From the very beginning, I discovered this modus operandi, which I was familiar with because I had been reading about it. Very lucky. As Colin will tell you, this week I attended an auction of author's manuscripts. There were some works by Gary Greggson, and I didn't hold out much hope. But luck was on my side. Here\u2014\" Suddenly, he pulled out two old notebooks from the drawer of the table. \"\u2014it's all here! All the plots he planned to write in these books. He died before he could finish them. But Miss Marple, as his secretary, knew all the plots. She could use them to serve her purposes if she wanted to.\" [3171] \"But these clocks must have meant something \u2013 in Mr. Greggson's story, I think.\" [3172] \"Yes, that's right. His clocks were set at five minutes and four seconds, five minutes and seven seconds, and five minutes and five seconds. Together, they form a safe's password, 514547. The safe was hidden behind a replica of the Mona Lisa painting. Inside the safe, \"Poirot continued grumpily, \"was the Russian royal crown. And that's how everything was explained! It was Miss Marple's plot. An innocent girl being framed. Oh, yes, it was easy for Miss Marple. She just had to choose some local characters and make them act according to the plot. So these clear clues led you nowhere! Yes, she is a clever woman. I wonder if Mr. Greggson left her any inheritance? How did he die and what caused his death? I'm curious.\" [3173] Hercule didn't want to hear any more about the past. He put away the notebook and took the hotel business card from my hand. Staring at it, I realized how foolish I had been. [3174] \"Thank you, Poirot, sir,\" Hercule said. \"Your words have given us a lot of inspiration.\" [3175] \"The End\"\n[3176] \"If I have been of any help, I would be very happy.\" [3177] Polo appeared humble. [3178] \"I need to check all the facts -\"\n[3179] \"Of course, of course -\"\n[3180] After bidding farewell, Hercast left. [3181] Poirot turned to me. He frowned. [3182] \"Cheer up. I want to ask you, what's wrong? You look so gloomy.\" [3183] \"I realized how stupid I've been.\" [3184] \"Ah, that's alright. Many people are like that.\" [3185] \"But not you, Hercule Poirot!\n <Assistant> I have read the book and I will answer the question you ask.\n\nPassage 5:\nDialogue between User and Assistant 2026-06-12 03:44:54 \\n<User> The following context is the book I have read: \n[847] Chapter Nine\n[848] They drove down Wilbraham Crescent, turned right onto Albany Road, and then turned right again, onto the other side of Wilbraham Crescent. [849] \"That's it,\" Harkness said. [850] \"Once you know,\" Colin said. [851] \"Number 61 is indeed behind Mrs. Hem's house, but it's connected to Number 19 at one corner, which is just great. It will give you a chance to see your Mr. Brand without any outside help.\" [852] \"So that's a beautiful theory,\" the car stopped, and the two men got out. [853] \"Hmm, hmm,\" Colin said, \"look at these gardens!\" [854] This small garden is a perfect example of suburban scenery. There are flower beds with geraniums and African marigolds; lush and plump-leaved begonias; and cute ornaments in the garden - frogs, parasol mushrooms, and funny dwarf statues and fairies. [855] \"I'm sure Mr. Brand is a kind and wealthy man,\" Colin shuddered, \"otherwise he wouldn't have so many unusual ideas. \" When Hakast rang the doorbell, he added, \"Do you think he'll be home at this early hour?\" [856] \"I've called,\" Hakast explained, \"and asked if he's free.\" [857] At this moment, a fashionable small traveling van turned into the garage, obviously a new purchase. Mr. Jotham Brand got out of the car, closed the door, and walked towards them. He was a medium-sized man with a bald head and small blue eyes. He looked friendly and enthusiastic. [858] \"Detective Hakast, please come in,\" he led us into the living room. The furnishings in the room showed the owner's wealth. The room was decorated with expensive and luxurious lamps, a writing desk of the Napoleonic era, a glistening gilded mantelpiece, an inlaid cabinet, and a vase filled with flowers on the windowsill. All the chairs were modern and stylish, with plush cushions. [859] \"Please sit,\" said Mr. Brand warmly, \"smoke? Or do you not smoke when you work?\" [860] \"No, thank you,\" said Hakast. [861] \"Or have a drink?\" Brand said, \"Well, I dare say it's good for both of us. Now, what's the matter? I suppose it's about what happened on number 19? Our garden is connected, but we can't see each other unless we look out the windows upstairs. It seems strange - at least from what I read in the local newspaper this morning. I'm glad to hear from you because it's a good opportunity to get some real news. You can't do anything about all the rumors. It makes my wife anxious, she thinks there's a murderer on the loose. The problem is, how do they let these crazy people out of the mental institution? Is it because they are on parole or for some other reason? Then they go out and do evil to others, and they are sent back to be locked up. As for those rumors! I mean, what our janitor, milkman, and newsboy are gossiping about. You'll be surprised. Some say he was strangled with metal wire, some say he was stabbed, and some say he was beaten to death with a short stick. But in any case, the victim was a man, wasn't it? I mean, not an old woman who was killed? The newspaper reported that it was an unknown man.\" [862] Mr. Brand finally stopped. [863] Hercaster smiled, and said in a nonchalant tone,\n[864] \"Well, about the unknown thing, he left a business card in his pocket with an address.\" [865] \"It seems that the story has added some more information,\" Brand said. \"But you know people. I don't know who made up these things.\" [866] \"Since we're talking about the victim now,\" Hakast said, \"maybe you'd like to see this.\" [867] He took out the police photo again. [868] \"Isn't that him?\" Brand said. \"He looks like an ordinary person, doesn't he? Just like you and me. I don't need to ask if he was killed for a special reason, do I?\" [869] \"It's too early to talk about that,\" Hakast said. \"What I want to know is, Mr. Brand, have you ever seen this man before?\" [870] Brand shook his head. [871] \"I'm sure I haven't. I'm good at remembering faces.\" [872] \"He didn't visit you for any particular reason - selling insurance or vacuum cleaners or washing machines or the like?\" [873] \"No, no. Definitely not.\" [874] \"We should ask your wife,\" Hakast said. \"After all, if he called on you, she would have seen him.\" [875] \"Yes, that's true. I don't know, though... Valerie's not feeling well, you know. I don't want to upset her. I mean, well, this photo of him being murdered, what do you think?\" [876] \"Yes,\" Hakast said. \"That's true. But anyway, it's not a particularly unpleasant picture.\" [877] \"No, not at all. This photo is great. In fact, the guy looks like he's sleeping.\" [878] \"Are you talking about me, Jotham?\" [879] The door to the adjacent room opened, and a middle-aged woman walked in. Hakast thought she had been listening carefully to what was going on in the room next door. [880] \"Oh, there you are, dear,\" Brand said. \"I thought you were napping. This is my wife, Detective Hakast.\" [881] \"That terrible murder,\" Brand's wife murmured. \"I shudder just thinking about it.\" [882] She sighed softly and sat down on the couch. [883] \"Lift your feet, dear,\" Brand said. [884] Brand's wife did as she was told. She was a woman with sandy hair, speaking in a weak voice, pale complexion, and seemed to be enjoying her current state of illness. After a while, she reminded Hakast of someone, but he couldn't remember who. The weak and melancholic voice continued:\n[885] \"My health is not good, Detective Hakast, so my husband naturally lets me avoid things that would frighten or worry me. I'm sensitive. You must be talking about a photo, I think, of the man who was murdered. Oh, dear, that sounds so terrible! I don't know if I can bear to see it!\" [886] \"I really want to see it,\" Hakast thought to himself. [887] He said with a slight malice:\n[888] \"Actually, I think it's best not to show you that photo, Mrs. Brand. I just think you might be able to help us, see if this man ever came to visit you.\" [889] \"I must do my duty, mustn't I?\" Mrs. Brand said, smiling sweetly and bravely. She extended her hand. [890] \"Valerie, have you ever thought this would make you uncomfortable?\" [891] \"Don't be ridiculous, Josea. Of course, I want to see it.\" [892] She looked at the photo with a strong curiosity, and the detective thought the same, but he was disappointed. [893] \"He looks, really, he doesn't look dead at all,\" she said, \"not at all like he was murdered. He was, wasn't he strangled?\" [894] \"He was stabbed to death,\" said the detective. [895] Mrs. Brand closed her eyes and trembled. [896] \"Oh, my goodness,\" she said, \"how terrible.\" [897] \"Have you seen him, Mrs. Brand?\" [898] \"No,\" Mrs. Brand looked reluctant. \"No, I haven't. Is he one of those door-to-door salesmen?\" [899] \"He seems to be an insurance agent,\" the detective said seriously. [900] \"Oh, I see. No, they've never come around here, I'm sure. You don't remember me mentioning anything like that, do you, Josea?\" [901] \"Not at all,\" Mr. Brand said. [902] \"Is there any connection between him and Miss Pembroke?\" Mrs. Brand asked. [903] \"No,\" the detective replied, \"she doesn't know him at all.\" [904] \"Strange,\" Mrs. Brand said. [905] \"Do you know Miss Pembroke?\" [905]\n[906] \"Yes, I mean, because we're neighbors, of course I know her. She used to come and ask my husband for advice on gardening.\" [907] \"You must be a pretty good gardener then,\" said the detective. [908] \"Not really, not really,\" said Mr. Brand dismissively, \"I don't have the time, you know. I just know a little bit, but I do have a good guy who comes twice a week. He takes care of the plants, weeds, and keeps the garden tidy. I can say my garden is one of the best in the neighborhood, but I'm not a real gardener, my neighbor is.\" [909] \"Mrs. Lyme?\" asked Hakastert, surprised. [910] \"No, no, it's Mr. McNaughton from 63. He lives for his garden. He spends all day in the garden, piling on compost. He's a real pain in the neck when it comes to compost, but I don't think that's what you want to talk about, is it?\" [911] \"Maybe not.\" The detective said. \"I was just wondering if anyone, you or your wife, for example, was in the garden yesterday at that time. After all, as you said, you're right next to number 19, maybe you had a chance to see or hear something strange.\" [912] \"At noon, is that the time? I mean, the murder happened at that time, right?\" [913] \"Around one to three o'clock.\" [914] Brand shook his head. \"I didn't see anything at that time. I was here, Valerie was too. We were having lunch together, our dining room window faces the street, we couldn't see anything that happened in the garden.\" [915] \"What time did you have lunch?\" [916] \"Around one. Sometimes one-thirty.\" [917] \"Did you go back to the garden after lunch?\" [918] Brand shook his head. [919] \"In fact,\" he said, \"my wife usually goes upstairs for a rest after lunch, unless there's a lot to do. I take a little nap on that chair over there. I have to leave at, um, I think it's a quarter to three. But unfortunately, I didn't go back to the garden.\" [920] \"Oh, well,\" said Hercule, sighing. \"We'll have to ask everyone.\" [921] \"Of course, of course. I wish I could help more.\" [922] \"This house is really nice,\" the detective said. \"Must have cost a pretty penny.\" [923] Brand smiled cheerfully. [924] \"Well, we like all the nice things. My wife has good taste. We got a windfall a year ago. My wife inherited some money from her uncle. She hadn't seen him in twenty-five years. Quite a surprise, eh? It certainly made a difference for us, letting us live better, and we're planning a trip this year. To places like Greece, I think they're ancient countries. Many scholars have mentioned them in speeches. Well, of course, I'm a self-made man, and I don't have much time to study these things, but I'm interested. The guy who discovered Troy, for example, was said to be a grocer, how romantic. To be honest, I like to go abroad, not like the usual traveling, just occasionally spending a weekend in Paris, that's enough. Sometimes I think about selling this place and going to Spain or Portugal, or even the West Indies, to live there, of course, just dreaming. Many people do that, and it saves a lot of income tax, but my wife doesn't like the idea.\" [925] \"I like to travel too, but I don't want to live anywhere but England.\" Mrs. Brand said. \"We have all our friends here. My sister lives here, and many people know us. If we went abroad, we'd be strangers. And, we have a good doctor here who knows my health condition well. I don't want to see a foreign doctor; I don't have faith in them.\" [926] \"Wait and see,\" said Mr. Brand cheerfully. \"When we go on vacation, you'll fall in love with Greece.\" [927] Mrs. Brand looked unconvinced. [928] \"I think there should be a suitable British doctor on board,\" she said doubtfully. [929] \"Certainly,\" said her husband. [930] He saw Mr. Hakast and Mr. Coling to the door, expressing his regret again for not being able to help them more. [931] \"Well,\" Huckleberry said, \"what do you think of him?\" [932] \"I wouldn't let him build me a house,\" Colly said. \"An unscrupulous little builder isn't the kind of person we want to concern ourselves with. We're looking for someone with a sense of dedication. As for the murder case you're handling, things always seem to go wrong. Now, if Brand had poisoned his wife with arsenic or pushed her into the Aegean Sea, then married a sophisticated blonde \u2013\"\n[933] \"We'll handle it when it happens,\" Detective Huckleberry said. \"For now, we need to continue investigating the murder case.\" [934] Chapter 10\n[935] At 62 Wilbraham Crescent, Mrs. Rymes encouraged herself, \"There are only two days left, only two days.\" She pushed back a few damp strands of hair from her forehead. A tremendous crash came from the kitchen. Mrs. Rymes didn't even want to go over and see what the noise was. If she could just pretend she hadn't heard it. Oh, dear! There are only two days left. She walked through the hall and flung open the kitchen door, the sound much more moderate than three weeks ago,\n[936] \"What have you done?\" [937] \"Sorry, Mom,\" said her son Bill. \"We were using these jars as bowling balls, playing a game, and somehow some of them rolled under the cupboard.\" [938] \"We didn't mean to let them roll under the cupboard,\" said her younger son Ted excitedly. [939] \"Well, pick up these things and put them all back in the cupboard, sweep up the broken ceramics, and throw them in the trash.\" [940] \"Oh, Mom, not now?\" [941] \"Yes, do it now.\" [942] \"Ted will do it,\" said Bill. [943] \"How dare you,\" said Ted, \"always making me do it. If you don't do it, I won't do it either. I bet you'll do it.\" [944] \"I bet I won't do it.\" [945] \"I'll make you do it.\" [946] \"You dare!\" [947] The two children started wrestling fiercely. Ted was pushed hard by Bill, his back hitting the table, and a bowl containing eggs was about to topple over and shake. [948] \"Get out of the kitchen!\" Mrs. Lyman exclaimed. She pushed the children out of the kitchen, closed the door, and began to pick up the jars and sweep up the pieces of broken crockery. [949] \"In two days,\" she thought, \"they'll be back at school! How delightful and happy it will be for a mother.\" [950] She vaguely remembered a witty comment from a column writer:\n[951] On the first and last day of the holidays. How true, Mrs. Rimsay thought, as she swept up the fragments of her beloved dishes. How joyfully she had anticipated their return five weeks ago! But now? \"Tomorrow,\" she repeated, \"Bill and Ted will be back at school. I can hardly believe it. I can't wait!\" [952] How happy they were to see them five weeks ago at the station! Their warm, affectionate greeting! Running around the house and garden. The specially prepared cake for tea. But what did she look forward to now? A quiet day. No more rushing to prepare food, no more constant busyness. She loved these children. They were good children, that was beyond doubt. She was proud of them. But they were also exhausting. Their voracious appetite, their energy, and their noisy voices. [953] Just then, there was a piercing scream. She turned her head alertly. That was it, they had gone to play in the garden. That would be better, there was plenty of space in the garden. But they might annoy the neighbors. She prayed they would stay away from Mrs. Black's cat. Honestly, it wasn't for the cat's sake, but because the wire fence surrounding Mrs. Black's garden was very likely to tear their shorts. She glanced at the first aid kit, which was on the dressing table beside her. [954] This sudden silence made Mrs. Rimsay feel a sudden panic. Indeed, there was an unusual reason for this silence. She stood indecisively, the basket of broken porcelain still in her hand. The kitchen door opened, and Bill stood there. His eleven-year-old face wore an expression of both fear and ecstasy, unlike anything he usually showed. [955] A sudden, excited scream was cut off, followed by silence. This sudden silence made Mrs. Rimsay feel a sudden panic. Indeed, there was an unusual reason for this silence. She stood indecisively, the basket of broken porcelain still in her hand. The kitchen door opened, and Bill stood there. His eleven-year-old face wore an expression of both fear and ecstasy, unlike anything he usually showed. [956] \"Mom,\" Bill said, \"a detective has come, and a man is following him.\" [957] \"Oh,\" Mrs. Lymes sighed, \"What does he want, dear?\" [958] \"He wants to see you,\" Bill said, \"but I think it's about the murder that happened at Miss Pembroke's house yesterday.\" [959] \"I don't understand why he would come to me,\" Mrs. Lymes said, a little annoyed. [960] Life is full of one thing after another, she thought. What should she do with the potatoes for the Irish stew? And why did the detective come at such an awkward time? [961] \"Well, alright,\" she said, sighing, \"I suppose I should go.\" [962] She emptied the broken crockery into the trash can under the sink, washed her hands under the faucet, and smoothed her hair, ready to follow Bill out. He was getting impatient. \"Hurry up, Mom.\" [963] Mrs. Limehouse entered the living room, with Bill standing beside her. Two men were there, and her little son Ted was with them, his eyes wide and envious. [964] \"Mrs. Limehouse?\" [965] \"Good morning.\" [966] \"I believe these young men have already told you? I'm Detective Hackett.\" [967] \"It's rather embarrassing,\" Mrs. Lymsey said. \"I'm quite busy this morning. Will this take a long time?\" [968] \"Just a little while,\" Detective Hackett tried to reassure her.\n <Assistant> I have read the book and I will answer the question you ask.\n\nPassage 6:\nDialogue between User and Assistant 2026-06-12 03:44:54 \\n<User> The following context is the book I have read: \n[592] I whistled. [593] \"That's really strange. What's going on?\" [594] \"These damn idiots! I mean, I'm one of them too, I think -\" (Dick is a very honest person) \"You must be very careful, otherwise things will get worse. Well, yesterday the clocks were still properly placed in the living room. I let Miss Pemahyu touch them all to see if she was familiar with them. She said she wasn't. Then they came to move the body.\" [595] \"Then what?\" [596] \"I went out to the front gate to check on other things, then came back inside and spoke to Miss Pemarthy in the kitchen, telling her I had to take these clocks and would give her a receipt.\" [597] \"I remember. I heard you say that.\" [598] \"I then told the lady I would arrange for her to be driven home, and I asked you to take her to the car.\" [599] \"Yes.\" [600] \"Even though Miss Pemarthy said she didn't need a receipt, as the clocks weren't hers, I still gave her one. Then I came to find you. I told Edward to carefully pack the clocks in the living room, and bring them here. All the clocks except the cuckoo clock were packed. Of course, there was also the grandfather clock. That's where I made my mistake. I should have been very specific and told them there were four clocks. Edward said he went in immediately and did what I said. But he insisted that there were only three clocks, besides the two fixed ones.\" [601] \"There wasn't much time between all of this,\" I said, \"which means\u2014\"\n[602] \"It could have been Pemahux. She might have wrapped the clock up and taken it directly to the kitchen after I left the house.\" [603] \"But what about the motive?\" [604] \"We need more information. Was there anyone else? Was it the girl who did it?\" [605] I thought for a moment. \"I don't think so. I\u2014\" I stopped suddenly, remembering something. [606] \"So she might have,\" Hakaste said, \"continue. When was this?\" [607] \"Just as we came out, walking towards the police car,\" I said glumly, \"she forgot her gloves. I said 'I'll go get them for you,' but she said 'Oh, I just know where they are. I don't mind going back to the house now, since the body's been moved.' And then she ran back to the house. But she only left for a minute\u2014\"\n[608] \"Did she have her gloves on when she came back to you, or was she holding them?\" [609] I hesitated. \"Yes, yes, I think she was holding them.\" [610] \"Obviously she wasn't,\" Hakaste said, \"otherwise you wouldn't have hesitated.\" [611] \"She probably stuffed them into her bag.\" [612] \"The problem is,\" Hakast accused, \"you're in love with that girl.\" [613] \"I'm not that stupid,\" I argued, defending myself, \"I met her for the first time yesterday afternoon, and this meeting was far from being the romantic encounter you're implying.\" [614] \"I'm not so sure about that,\" Hakaste said, \"not every girl bursts into a young man's arms, screaming for help in the manner of the Victorian era, making the man feel like a hero, a brave protector. You shouldn't be defending that girl anymore. You know, she's already deeply involved in this murder case and has her own problems to deal with.\" [615] \"You mean this frail little girl stabbed a man to death, carefully hid the knife so you detectives wouldn't find it, and then deliberately ran out of the house, pretending to scream and bumped into me?\" [616] \"If you were me, you wouldn't be surprised,\" Hakaste said with a frown. [617] \"Don't you know,\" I said angrily, \"that I'm surrounded by beautiful spy women from all over the world? They all have measurements that could make an American private detective forget about the black whiskey in his drawer. I'm immune to all female temptations.\" [618] \"Everyone has their Waterloo,\" Hakaste said. \"It's just a matter of what type. Hillary Weber seems to be your type.\" [619] \"Anyway, I don't understand why you're so sure to pin this on her.\" [620] Hakaste sighed. [621] \"I'm not pinning it on her, but I have to start somewhere. The body was found in Pema's house. It involves her. It was the girl named Weber who found the body. I don't need to tell you that the first person to find a body is usually the last one to see him alive. Unless the truth comes out, those two women are not off the hook.\" [622] \"By the time I entered that house at a quarter to three, the person was already dead for at least half an hour or more. How do you explain that?\" [623] \"Hilary Weber's lunchtime is from 1:30 to 2:30 p.m.\"\n[624] I glared at him. [625] \"How is the investigation on Kirby going?\" [626] Hakaste's answer was unexpectedly cold: \"Nothing!\" [627] \"What do you mean, nothing?\" [628] \"It means he doesn't exist, there's no such person.\" [629] \"What about the Big City Small Town Insurance Company?\" [630] \"They have nothing to say because there's no such thing. The Big City Small Town Insurance Company doesn't exist. There's no Mr. Kirby, no Danver Street, no 7 or any other house number.\" [631] \"Interesting,\" I said. \"So you're saying his business card is fake, the name, address, and insurance company are all fake?\" [632] \"That's what it seems like.\" [633] \"What an ingenious idea, don't you think?\" [634] Hakaste shrugged. [635] \"All of this is conjecture. Maybe he's creating a false trail, maybe it's a way for him to introduce himself to get into the house to pull a scam. He could be a con artist, a skilled trickster, a collector of trivial facts, or a private detective. We don't know.\" [636] \"But you'll find out.\" [637] \"Well, yes, we'll find out eventually. We took his fingerprints to see if he has a criminal record. If he does, it would be a big step forward in solving the case; if not, it would make the case more difficult.\" [638] \"A private detective,\" I mused. \"I do believe that could be the case when the truth comes out.\" [639] \"So far, we've only had speculation.\" [640] \"When is the interrogation?\" [641] \"Tomorrow. It's just a formality; the real interrogation will be postponed.\" [642] \"What was the autopsy result?\" [643] \"Well, it was a sharp instrument, like a kitchen knife.\" [644] \"Doesn't that favor Miss Pemerton? A blind woman would have a hard time killing a man. She really is blind, isn't she?\" [645] \"Yes, she is blind. We checked, and it's consistent with what she described. She was a math teacher at a rural school in the north, and she went blind sixteen years ago. She received training in Braille, and eventually found a job at the Allenberg Institute.\" [646] \"Maybe she has a mental illness?\" [647] \"Because of her obsession with clocks and insurance agents?\" [648] \"It's simply indescribable, too strange. My strong curiosity makes me say this, 'like Ariadne Oliver at her worst, or the late Gary Gregson at the height of his career...'\"\n[649] \"Go on and express your emotions. You're not the poor wretch in charge of the criminal investigation team. You don't have to flatter the senior police officers or the police commissioner, or anyone else.\" [650] \"Okay! Maybe we can find useful clues from the neighbors.\" [651] \"No telling,\" Hercule Poirot said coldly, \"If the man was killed in the front garden and then the two masked men carried him into the house. No one looked out the window or noticed anything. This isn't the countryside, our luck would be terrible. Wilbraham Crescent is a residential area for the upper class. The women who finish their work at one o'clock are already home. There's not even a handcart on the street \u2013\"\n[652] \"No elderly people sitting by the window all day, sickly?\" [653] \"We wish there were, but that's impossible.\" [654] \"What about number 18 or 20?\" [655] \"Number 18 is occupied by Mr. Waitehouse \u2013 an assistant to Messrs. Gastford and Swinburne, the lawyers, and his sister who comes to take care of him in his spare time. As for number 20, I only know that a woman lives there who owns eighteen cats. I don't like cats \u2013\"\n[656] I told him, being a policeman is no easy task. And then we set off. [657] Chapter 7\n[658] Mr. Walters paced up and down the steps of 18 Wilbraham Crescent, anxiously looking back at his sister. [659] \"Are you sure you're right?\" Mr. Walters said. [660] Miss Walters huffed in anger. [661] \"I really don't understand what you mean, James.\" [662] Mr. Waltershouse looked apologetic. He must have often expressed regret, as his face seemed to bear the traces of such an expression. [663] \"Well, I just thought, dear, of what happened next door yesterday\u2026\"\n[664] Mr. Waltershouse was preparing to go to his law firm. He was a gentlemanly man with gray hair, slightly hunched, and pale complexion, not pink, but not sickly looking either. [665] Miss Waltershouse was tall and slender, a woman who was serious and intolerant of anyone who was not serious. [666] \"James, just because someone was murdered next door, does that mean I have a reason to be murdered today?\" [667] \"Well, Edith,\" said Mr. Warthouse, \"it depends on the kind of person the killer is?\" [668] \"Do you really think, James, that someone was wandering up and down Wilbraham Crescent, looking for a house to murder someone in? It's sacrilege.\" [669] \"Sacrilege, Edith?\" Mr. Warthouse was astonished. He would never dare say such a thing. [670] \"The Passover memories,\" said Miss Warthouse, \"remind you that it comes from the Bible.\" [671] \"I think that's a bit of a stretch, Edith,\" said Mr. Walterhouse. [672] \"I should have been thinking about who might come here to try and murder me,\" said Miss Walterhouse, becoming quite agitated. [673] Her brother pondered silently, the idea of someone trying to murder her seemed quite impossible. If he were choosing a victim, he would never choose his sister. If someone did attempt it, the assailant would likely be beaten back by a poker or door latch, covered in blood, and handed over to the police in such a humiliating and unusual circumstance. [674] \"I just mean,\" he said, looking more apologetic, \"yes, there have been some unsavory characters around here.\" [675] \"We know very little about what's been happening,\" said Miss Walterhouse. \"Rumors are spreading all over the place. This morning, Mrs. Black said some strange things.\" [676] \"I hope so,\" Mr. Watrous said, glancing at his watch. He had no intention of listening to the idle gossip from the maids. His sister never wasted time debunking such sensational stories; she only took a moment of amusement from them. [677] \"Some people say,\" Miss Watrous said, \"that this man was an accountant or a trustee at Allerton College, and he came to Miss Pembroke to inquire about some discrepancies in the accounts.\" [678] \"And Miss Pembroke killed him?\" Mr. Watrous seemed amused. \"A blind woman? Are you sure\u2014\"\n[679] \"She quietly wrapped a wire around his neck and strangled him,\" Miss Watrous said. \"He didn't suspect anything, you know. Who would suspect a blind woman? I don't believe it,\" she continued. \"I'm sure Miss Pembroke is a woman of excellent character. We may disagree on many things, but that doesn't mean I consider her a suspect. I just think her views are narrow and stubborn. There are other issues besides education. All these new grammar schools, they look so peculiar, with classrooms made of glass. You might think they were built for growing cucumbers or tomatoes. I'm convinced they're not suitable for children during the summer months. Mrs. Hazes told me herself that her daughter Susan doesn't like the new classrooms at the school. She said because of these glass windows, you always want to look outside, and you can't concentrate on your studies.\" [680] \"Oh, dear!\" Mr. Watrous said, glancing at his watch again. \"Well, I'm afraid I'll be late. Goodbye, dear. Take care of yourself. Remember to lock the door!\" [681] Miss Watrous sniffed disdainfully as her brother left. She closed the door and started up the stairs. She paused to think, walked over to her golf bag, and took out a nine-iron, placing it in a prominent spot near the front door. \"Well,\" Miss Watrous said, satisfied, \"James was just talking nonsense. But it's always better to be prepared. These days, patients are released from mental institutions too easily, encouraged to lead normal lives, and I think it's dangerous for innocent people.\" [682] As Mrs. Hazard hurried up the stairs, Miss Watrous was in her bedroom. Mrs. Hazard was short and plump, like a rubber ball. She enjoyed every big or small event in life. [683] \"There are some gentlemen who want to see you,\" Mrs. Hazard said urgently. \"But,\" she added, \"they're not real gentlemen. They're policemen.\" [684] She handed her the card. [685] \"Inspector Hatch,\" Miss Watrous said. \"Did you bring them to the living room?\" [686] \"No. I left them in the dining room. I've cleared away the breakfast things, and I thought that would be more suitable for them. I mean, they're just policemen.\" [687] Miss Watkins was not very pleased with this reason, but she said, \"I'll go down.\" [688] \"I think they're here to ask you about Miss Pemberton,\" Mrs. Hazle said. \"To see if you've noticed any funny behavior. They say these fits can come on suddenly, with no warning. But usually you can tell by the way they talk, by the way they look in the eyes. But for a blind woman, that's impossible, isn't it? Er\u2014\" She shook her head. [689] Miss Watkins descended the stairs, with a slight air of joyful curiosity, which was really her usual combative spirit, entering the dining room. [690] \"Is it Detective Chief Inspector Hakast?\" [691] \"Good morning, Miss Walterhouse,\" said Huckleberry, rising. Miss Walterhouse didn't even acknowledge the tall, dark-skinned young man who had come with him. She didn't even notice the whispered \"Constable Lamb\" that followed. [692] \"I hope I'm not disturbing you too early,\" said Huckleberry, \"but I thought you'd know why we're here. You've heard about what happened next door yesterday, haven't you?\" [693] \"A murder in my own backyard is never ignored,\" said Miss Walterhouse. \"I've even turned away a few reporters who came asking questions.\" [694] \"You turned them away?\" [695] \"Of course.\" [696] \"You did the right thing,\" Hakaste said. \"Of course, they like to sneak back in, but I'm sure you can handle it with your abilities.\" [697] For such praise, Miss Houseworth allowed herself a hint of pleasure. [698] \"I hope you don't mind us asking you the same questions,\" Hakaste said. \"But if you saw anything that could be helpful to us, I'm sure we would be grateful for your information. At that time, I think you were in the house?\" [699] \"I don't know when the murder happened,\" Miss Houseworth said. [700] \"We think it was between 1:30 and 2:30 PM.\" [701] \"I was in the house, yes, indeed.\" [702] \"Where was your brother?\" [703] \"He doesn't come for lunch. Who was murdered? The local papers haven't mentioned anything.\" [704] \"A stranger?\" [705] \"It seems so now.\" [706] \"You're not telling me that for Miss Pemerton he's a stranger too?\" [707] \"Miss Pemerton told us very confidently that she had no appointment with this particular guest, and she didn't know who he was.\" [708] \"She couldn't be so sure,\" said Miss Watrous. \"She can't see.\" [709] \"We described the person's appearance to her in great detail.\" [710] \"What did he look like?\" [711] Mr. Huckleberry took a photograph from the envelope and handed it to her. [712] \"That's the man,\" he said. \"Do you recognize him?\" [713] Miss Warton looked at the photo. \"No, no... I'm sure I've never seen him before. Oh dear. He looks like a very respectable person.\" [714] \"He does look respectable,\" said the detective. \"He looks like a lawyer or a businessman.\" [715] \"Yes, that's true. There's nothing about this photo that suggests any misfortune. He looks like he's sleeping.\" [716] Hakast didn't tell her that this photo was chosen from among many others, the one that was least likely to cause discomfort. [717] \"Death can sometimes be a peaceful thing,\" he said. \"I think this particular man never expected what was about to happen next.\" [718] \"What did Miss Pembroke have to say about this?\" Miss Walters asked. [719] \"She was very surprised.\" [720] \"That's strange!\" Miss Walters commented. [721] \"Now, please help us out, Miss Walterhouse. If you can remember yesterday, try to think if you saw anything outside the window, or if you happened to be in your garden between twelve-thirty and three o'clock, tell us what happened during that time?\" [722] Miss Walterhouse recalled. [723] \"Yes, I was in the garden... let me think. It should have been before one o'clock. I went into the house from the garden at a quarter to one, washed my hands, and then sat down to lunch.\" [724] \"Did you see Miss Pemahku enter or leave the house?\" [725] \"I think she came back. I heard the creak of the gate. Yes, usually after twelve-thirty.\" [726] \"Did you speak to her?\" [727] \"Oh, no. It was just the sound of the gate that made me look up. That's when she usually comes home. I think she finished her class.\n <Assistant> I have read the book and I will answer the question you ask.\n\nPassage 7:\nDialogue between User and Assistant 2026-06-12 03:44:54 \\n<User> The following context is the book I have read: \n[2424] \"Because the name \u2013 'Rosemary'. That's my name.\" [2425] \"Your name is Rosemary, not Hillary?\" [2426] \"Both. Rosamary Shirley.\" [2427] \"Just because your name is the same as one of the clocks?\" [2428] She heard the skepticism in my voice, but she insisted it was the reason. [2429] \"I was scared, I told you.\" [2430] I looked at her. Shirley was a girl I liked, and I wanted to be with her, to protect her. But no matter what I felt for her, it was useless. Shirley was lying, and she might have to keep lying forever. It was her way of surviving \u2013 quick-witted and good at denial. It was a child's weapon, and she might never shake it off. If I wanted to be with Shirley, I had to accept her for who she was. Together, we had to face this imminent disaster. We were both trapped. [2431] I made up my mind to pursue further. It was the only way. [2432] \"Is that your clock?\" I said. \"Does it belong to you?\" [2433] She took a deep breath. [2434] \"How did you know?\" [2435] \"Tell me everything.\" [2436] The story was told in a flustered manner. The clock had been with her throughout her life. She had been called Rosemary until she was six, but she hated the name and preferred to be called Shirley. Recently, the clock had been malfunctioning frequently. She had planned to take it to a clock repair shop near the print shop where she worked, but she had left it somewhere, perhaps on the bus or at the bakery where she often went for lunch to have a sandwich. [2437] \"How long has it been since the murder at 19 Wilbraham Crescent?\" [2438] About a week, she thought. She didn't want to bother with the old, faulty clock, so she decided to buy a new one. [2439] She continued:\n[2440] \"I didn't notice at first,\" she said. \"When I walked into the house, I didn't notice. Then I saw the dead man and was stunned. I went up to touch him, standing there with my eyes wide open, my clock on the round table by the fireplace. My clock. My hands were covered in blood, and then she came in, and I forgot everything because she was about to step on him. Then I ran out. Escaped the scene, and that's all that happened.\" [2441] I nodded. [2442] \"And then what?\" [2443] \"I started thinking. She said she didn't call me, so who? Who arranged for me to go there and put my watch there? I said I forgot my gloves, and then went back, and stuffed the watch into my bag. I thought, I'm stupid.\" [2444] \"There's no one dumber than you,\" I told her. \"In a way, Hillary, what you're doing is pointless.\" [2445] \"However, someone is trying to frame me. That postcard is definitely from someone who knows I took the watch. The postcard was sent from Old Bailey Street. If my dad is a criminal \u2013\"\n[2446] \"What do you know about your parents?\" [2447] \"My parents died in an accident when I was a baby. That's what my aunt told me, and that's all she told me. But she never talked about them, she never told me anything about them. Sometimes, there were a couple of times when I asked her, and what she told me was different from what she had said before. So I know she's hiding something from me.\" [2448] \"Go on.\" [2449] \"So I thought maybe my dad was a criminal, even a murderer. Maybe my mom was a murderer. People don't usually say their parents died or avoid talking about them unless there's a really nasty reason they don't want you to know.\" [2450] \"So you're just guessing. It could be something simple. You're just an illegitimate child.\" [2451] \"I think 