d the bill, and stood up. [2465] \"Don't worry,\" I said (which is one of the least sincere things one can say in English or any other language), \"Colin Lamb Private Services will continue to work. You'll be fine, we'll get married, and live happily ever after, just a year from now. By the way, \" I said, unable to stop myself, even though I knew a more romantic ending would be better, but Colin Lamb's insatiable curiosity drove me to do so. \"What did you do with that clock? Did you hide it in your sock drawer?\" [2466] She hesitated for a moment, then said,\n[2467] \"I threw it in the trash bin of the neighbor's house next door.\" [2468] I was somewhat surprised. That was neat and clean. It shows that she's smart. Maybe, I underestimated Hilary. [2469] Chapter Twenty-Four\n[2470] Colin Blue's Narrative\n[2471] 1\n[2472] After Shira left, I went straight back to the Clarendon, packed my bags, and handed them to the staff. This hotel is particularly attentive to whether you will check out before noon. [2473] Then I set off. As I passed by the police station, I hesitated for a moment and decided to go in. I found Hakast there, who was frowning and looking at a letter in his hand. [2474] \"I'm leaving again tonight, Dick,\" I said. \"Back to London.\" [2475] He looked up at me thoughtfully. [2476]\"Do you want to hear some advice?\" he asked. [2477]\"No,\" I replied immediately. [2478]He didn't pay attention to me. People who want to give advice usually act this way. [2479]\"You should leave and stay away if you know what's best for you.\" [2480]\"No one can judge what's best for others.\" [2481] \"I'll keep my opinion.\" [2482] \"I want to tell you something, Dick. After completing the current task, I plan to resign. At least, I hope to resign.\" [2483] \"Why?\" [2484] \"I feel like a Victorian-era old priest. I'm very suspicious.\" [2485] \"Give yourself some time.\" [2486] I'm not sure what he meant by that sentence. I asked him why he seemed so troubled. [2487] \"Look at this.\" He handed me the letter he was reading. [2488] Dear Sir,\n[2489] I just thought of something. You asked if there were any obvious features about my husband, and I said no. But I was mistaken. There is actually a scar on the back of his left ear. One time, when he was shaving, our dog lunged at him, and he got a cut from the razor. He had to get several stitches. It was a minor incident, so I didn't remember it that day. [2490] Yours sincerely,\n[2491] Melina Rivale\n[2492] \"Her handwriting is elegant,\" I said, \"although I've never been a fan of purple ink. Does the deceased have any scars?\" [2493] \"He does have a scar. Right where she said it was.\" [2494] \"Didn't she see it when she identified the body?\" [2495] Hakaste shook his head. [2496] \"It was covered by his ear. You have to pull it forward to see it.\" [2497] \"Well, that's settled then. It's solid evidence. What else are you worrying about?\" [2498] Hakast sighed, saying the case was like a devil. He asked if I would visit my French or Belgian friend in London. [2499] \"Maybe. What's up?\" [2500] \"I mentioned him to the county police chief, and he said he was impressed \u2013 the Girl Scout murder case [1]. If he's willing to come here, I'd be more than happy to welcome him.\" [2501] \"I'm afraid not,\" I said. \"This man rarely leaves his house.\" [2502] 2\n[2503] At a quarter past twelve, I rang the doorbell at 62 Wilbraham Crescent. Mrs. Lamech opened the door. She barely looked up at me. [2504] \"What is it?\" she said. [2505] \"Can I talk to you? I came here ten days ago. You may not remember.\" [2506] She looked at me closely for a moment, her eyebrows slightly furrowed. [2507] \"You came here, with the detective, didn't you?\" [2508] \"Yes, Mrs. Limehouse. Can I come in?\" [2509] \"Of course. The police are never turned away. If I did turn you away, I'd be in trouble.\" [2510] She led me into the living room, sitting down roughly in a chair facing me. Her voice had sounded sharp before, but today, she looked tired, something she hadn't shown before. [2511] I said:\n[2512] \"It seems quite quiet here today... Your son has gone back to school, hasn't he?\" [2513] \"Yes, completely different.\" [2514] \"I'm not here about the murder case a few days ago, the girl killed in the phone booth. I'm not really involved with the police, you know.\" [2515] She looked somewhat surprised. [2516] \"I thought you were Detective, Lime, right?\" [2517] \"My name is Lime, yes, but I work in a completely different department.\" The lack of enthusiasm disappeared from Mrs. Lyme's expression. She stared at me directly and sternly. [2518] \"Oh,\" she said, \"well, what is it?\" [2519] \"Is your husband still abroad?\" [2520] \"Yes.\" [2521] \"He's been away for a long time, hasn't he, Mrs. Lam? And he's gone to a very far place?\" [2522] \"What do you know about this?\" [2523] \"Well, he's probably gone into the Iron Curtain country, isn't he?\" [2524] She fell silent for a moment, then spoke in a calm, emotionless voice,\n[2525] \"Yes. Yes, that's right.\" [2526] \"Do you know where he went?\" [2527] \"I know a little about it. He wanted me to meet him there.\" [2528] \"He must have been planning this for some time?\" [2529] \"I think so. He only told me recently.\" [2530] \"You don't agree with his ideas, do you?\" [2531] \"I used to, I think. But you know... you already know the details, don't you?\" [2532] \"You might be able to tell us some very useful information.\" [2533] She shook her head. [2534] \"No. I can't. It's not that I don't want to. You know, he's always been vague with me. I don't want to know. I hate all of this! When Michel told me he was planning to leave the country, go through customs, and go to Moscow, I wasn't surprised at all. Later, I had to make my own decision.\" [2535] \"So you decided not to support your husband's plan?\" [2536] \"No, not like that! My opinion is entirely personal. I think there will always be a woman going with him, of course, probably a fanatic. Some women might be extremely fanatical, but I'm not. I've always been a moderate leftist.\" [2537] \"Is your husband involved with the Larkin case?\" [2538] \"I don't know. Maybe. He never tells me these things.\" [2539] She suddenly looked at me intensely. [2540] \"We should clarify things, Mr. Lamb, or Mr. Wolf in sheep's clothing, whoever you are. I love my husband, and maybe I should go to Moscow with him, regardless of whether I agree with his political views. He wants me to take the children. I don't want to! That's just the way it is. So, I decided I have to stay with the children. Whether I'll ever see Michel again, I don't know. He has to choose his way of life, and I have to choose mine. But there's one thing I'm absolutely certain of: after he talked to me about this, I decided to let the children grow up in their own country. They're British. I want them to grow up like ordinary British children.\" [2541] \"I understand.\" [2542] \"That's all I know.\" Mrs. Lame said, standing up. [2543] Her attitude suddenly became much more determined. [2544] \"This must be a difficult choice,\" I said gently, \"I'm sorry for you.\" [2545] \"Me too.\" Perhaps my sympathy touched her. She smiled faintly. [2546] \"Perhaps you are right... I think your job has made you try to find more information from other people's expressions to know their feelings and thoughts. This must have been a fatal blow to me, but I have already gone through the toughest times... Now I have to make plans, what to do, where to go, whether to stay here or go somewhere else. I should look for a job. I used to do secretarial work. Maybe I should enroll in a refresher course to learn shorthand and typing.\" [2547] \"Well, don't work at Cavenish Typewriting and Stenography.\" [2548] \"Why?\" [2549] \"The girls who work there seem to have unfortunate things happen to them.\" [2550] \"If you think I know anything about that, you're wrong. I don't know.\" [2551] I wished her well and left. I didn't learn anything from her. I actually didn't have any high hopes. But someone has to finish the job. I guess. [2552] 3\n[2553] As I was about to leave the gate, I almost bumped into Mrs. McNaughton. She was carrying a shopping bag and swaying as she walked. [2554] \"Let me help you with that,\" I said, reaching out to take the shopping bag from her. She initially held on to it tightly, then leaned forward and looked at me closely, and finally let go. [2555] \"You're the young man from the police station,\" she said. \"I didn't recognize you at first.\" [2556] I carry the shopping bag to her door. She walks beside me, swaying unsteadily. I didn't expect the bag to be so heavy. I wonder what's inside. Pounds of potatoes? [2557] \"Don't ring the doorbell,\" she says. \"The door is not locked.\" [2558] There seems to be no locked doors on Wilbraham Crescent. [2559] \"How's your case going?\" she asks casually. \"He seemed to have married several times.\" [2560] I don't know what she's talking about. [2561] \"Who, I've been away lately,\" I explained. [2562] \"Oh, I see. You're on a case, I mean Mrs. Rival. I went to the trial. Such an ordinary woman. I couldn't help but think she didn't seem very sad about her husband's death.\" [2563] \"She hadn't seen him for fifteen years,\" I explained. [2564] \"Anne and I have been married for twenty years,\" she sighed. \"A long time. Since he stopped teaching at the university, he started a lot of gardening work... It's hard to know what people should do.\" [2565] Just then, Mr. MacNorton came out from the corner of the house, holding a shovel. [2566] \"Oh, you're back, dear. Let me take care of it\u2014\"\n[2567] \"Put it in the kitchen.\" Mrs. McNoughton said slyly, nudging me with her elbow. \"Just some cornflakes, eggs, and watermelon.\" She smiled cheerfully at her husband. [2568] I placed the shopping bag on the kitchen table. It clanged. [2569] Cornflakes, what a farce! My detective instincts kicked in. Underneath the tape, there were three bottles of whiskey. [2570] I now understood why Mrs. McNoughton was sometimes so cheerfully chatty and why she walked with a slight wobble. Perhaps that's why Mr. McNoughton resigned from his job. [2571] It was still early morning for this neighborhood as I walked down New Moon Street towards Albany Road. I met Mr. Brand along the way. Mr. Brand seemed to be in good spirits. He quickly recognized me. [2572] \"Are you okay? How's the case going? I heard they identified the body. He was pretty rough on his wife, I guess. By the way, sorry, you're not from around here, are you?\"\n <Assistant> I have read the book and I will answer the question you ask.\n\nPassage 8:\nDialogue between User and Assistant 2026-06-12 03:44:54 \\n<User> The following context is the book I have read: \nAs she reached into her pocket for her keys, a voice came from below, peering out from the corner near the door. [2848] \"A gentleman is waiting for you upstairs.\" [2849] \"Waiting for me?\" [2850] Mrs. Rivard sounded a bit surprised. [2851] \"Well, he can be called a gentleman. He's dressed well and looks good, but not Sir Algernon Phyllotaxis de Phyllotaxis, I'd say.\" [2852] Mrs. Rival finally found the keyhole and inserted it, turning it and going inside. [2853] The house smelled of a mixture of cabbage, fish, and eucalyptus. The smell of eucalyptus seemed to persist throughout the hall. Mrs. Rival held onto the railing and climbed upstairs. She pushed open the door to the first floor and walked in, then suddenly stopped and took a step back. [2854] \"Oh,\" she said, \"it's you.\" [2855] Detective Hakast stood up from his chair. [2856] \"Good evening, Mrs. Rilvall.\" [2857] \"What are you doing here?\" Mrs. Rilvall asked bluntly, unlike her usual manner. [2858] \"Well, I had to come to London for work,\" Detective Hakast said. \"I have a couple of things I'd like to discuss with you, so I came hoping to find you. That woman downstairs thought you'd be back soon.\" [2859] \"Oh,\" Mrs. Rilvall said, \"I don't understand, hmm.\" [2860] Detective Hakast pushed a chair forward. [2861] \"Please sit,\" he said politely. [2862] Their roles seemed reversed, with him as the host and her as the guest. Mrs. Rilvale sat, staring straight at him. [2863] \"What do you mean by a couple of things?\" she asked. [2864] \"Just small matters,\" Detective Chief Inspector Hakast said. \"Sudden small matters.\" [2865] \"You mean, about Harry?\" [2866] \"Exactly.\" [2867] \"Now look here,\" Mrs. Rival said in a challenging tone. A strong smell of liquor hit Detective Hakast's nose. \"My business with Harry is over. I don't want to talk about him. When I saw his picture in the paper, I went straight over and told you about him, didn't I? That was a long time ago, and I don't want to think about it anymore. I can't tell you any more. I've told you everything I can remember, and I don't want to hear any more about it.\" [2868] \"It's just a small detail,\" Detective Hakast said apologetically. [2869] \"Oh, well,\" Mrs. Rival said rudely. \"What is it? Start.\" [2870] \"You think that person is your husband or the person you had a marriage with fifteen years ago, right?\" [2871] \"By now, you should know the exact date, shouldn't you?\" [2872] \"More precise than I thought,\" Chief Inspector Hakakste said to himself, and continued. [2873] \"Yes, you're right. We checked. You got married on May 15, 1948.\" [2874] \"It's always unlucky to be a May bride, people say,\" Mrs. Rival said, sounding dejected. \"It didn't bring me any luck.\" [2875] \"Despite the time that's passed, you can still easily recognize your husband.\" [2876] Mrs. Rivard shifted uncomfortably. [2877] \"He doesn't look old.\" She said, \"Harry always took good care of himself.\" [2878] \"You can give us some additional confirmation information. You wrote to us about that scar, I think.\" [2879] \"Yes. It's behind his left ear. Here.\" Mrs. Rivard raised a hand and pointed to the spot. [2880] \"Behind his 'left ear'?\" Detective Hakast emphasized. [2881] \"Well\u2014\" she seemed to hesitate for a moment. \"Yes. I'm sure. Of course, sometimes left and right can get mixed up, can't they? But yes, on the left side of his neck. Here.\" She indicated the same spot with her hand again. [2882] \"You mean, when he shaved?\" [2883] \"Yes. The dog jumped up at him. We had a very lively dog. It would always jump at you. It was a very affectionate dog. It jumped at Harry, and he had a razor in his hand, and he cut deep into it. He bled a lot. The wound was sewed up, but the scar stayed there.\" [2884] \"That's a very valuable detail, Mrs. Rival. After all, sometimes people can look very much alike, especially after a long time has passed. But it's hard to find a man with the same scar as your husband in the same place. This discovery is quite trustworthy, isn't it? The case seems to have moved forward.\" [2885] \"I'm glad to have satisfied you,\" Mrs. Rival said. [2886] \"When did this razor accident happen?\" [2887] Mrs. Rival paused for a moment. [2888] \"It happened, uh, about six months after we got married. Yes, that's when it was. I remember that's when we started getting that dog.\" [2889] \"So, it happened in October or November of 1948, right?\" [2890] \"Yes.\" [2891] \"After your husband left you in 1951...\"\n[2892] \"I sent him away, not the other way around.\" Mrs. Rivard said with pride. [2893] \"I see. Anyway, since your husband left you in 1951, you haven't seen him until you saw his picture in the newspaper?\" [2894] \"Yes. I've already told you all of this.\" [2895] \"You're quite sure about that, Mrs. Rivard?\" [2896] \"Of course I am. I haven't seen Harry Castleman since that day until I saw the news of his death.\" [2897] \"That is quite strange, you know,\" Detective Hardcastle said. \"That is very strange.\" [2898] \"Why? What do you mean?\" [2899] \"Well, it's a rather odd thing. Scar tissue. Of course, it doesn't matter to us. But doctors will tell you a lot. They'll roughly tell you, you know, how long this scar has been on a man.\" [2900] \"I don't understand what you mean.\" [2901] \"Well, Mrs. Rivale, according to our forensic examination and the opinions of other doctors we've consulted, this scar tissue clearly shows that the wound couldn't have existed for more than five or six years.\" [2902] \"Nonsense,\" said Mrs. Rivale. \"I don't believe it. I... no one can be sure. Anyway, this isn't...\"\n[2903] \"So you see,\" Detective Hakast continued calmly, \"if this scar is only five or six years old, then the man isn't your husband, is he? He left you in 1951, and he didn't have this scar then.\" [2904] \"Maybe he didn't. But he's still Harry, anyway.\" [2905] \"But you haven't seen him since then, Mrs. Rivale. So, since you haven't seen him, how do you know he had this scar five or six years ago?\" [2906] \"You're confusing me,\" Mrs. Rival said. \"You're making me a little confused. Maybe it hasn't been as long as 1948. You can't remember everything. Anyway, I know Harry has that scar.\" [2907] \"I know,\" Detective Hakaste said, standing up. \"I think you should think carefully about what you're saying, Mrs. Rival. You don't want to get into trouble, do you?\" [2908] \"What do you mean, get into trouble?\" [2909] \"Well,\" Detective Hakaste almost apologized, \"perjury.\" [2910] \"Perjury? Me?\" [2911] \"Yes. It's a serious offense, you know. You might get into a dispute, even go to jail. Of course, you didn't swear in the inquest, but you might have to swear in other hearings about your testimony. So, um, I think you should think carefully about it, Mrs. Rivard. Maybe someone is telling you to tell us about this scar.\" [2912] Mrs. Rivard stood up. She straightened her body, and her eyes shone. In that moment, she looked almost dignified. [2913] \"I've never heard such nonsense in my life,\" she said. \"It's all made up. I'm just doing my duty as a citizen. I came to see you, to help you, to tell you everything I remember. If I made a mistake, I'm sure it's natural. After all, I know many, um, gentlemen friends, and sometimes I might get things mixed up. But I don't think I 'did' anything wrong. That man is Harry, and he has a scar on the back of his left ear, and I'm quite sure of it. Now, perhaps, Inspector Hakaste, you should leave, instead of staying here insinuating that I've been lying all along.\" [2914] Inspector Hakaste got up immediately. [2915] \"Goodnight, Mrs. Rivard,\" he said. \"You need to think it over. That's all.\" [2916] Mrs. Rival raised her head. Detective Hakast left through the door. As soon as he was gone, Mrs. Rival's demeanor changed. Her defiant posture collapsed. She looked both fearful and anxious. [2917] \"Give me trouble,\" she murmured. \"Give me trouble. I don't want to do this anymore. I want to, I want to, I don't want to get into trouble for anyone. Tell me lots of things, lie to me, deceive me. Disgusting, too disgusting.\" [2918] She paced back and forth, finally making up her mind. She picked up an umbrella from the corner and went out again. She walked to the end of the street, hesitating in front of a phone booth, and then went to a post office. She went inside, exchanged some coins, and then entered one of the phone booths. She dialed an inquiry number, requesting a transfer to a certain number. She stood there waiting until the call was connected. [2919] \"Connected, please speak.\" [2920] She spoke up. [2921] \"Hello... Oh, it's you. I'm Flo. No, I know you told me not to, but I have to. You never told me it would cause trouble. You just said that if this man was confirmed, it would be bad for you. I never thought I'd be caught up in a murder case... well, you'd say that, but anyway, you never told me... yes. I know. I think you're involved in this mess... well, I won't support it... it will become a \u2014 well, you know what I mean \u2014 an accessory, something like that. Anyway, it's against the facts, and I'm a little scared, I tell you... to write a letter, to tell them about the scar. Now this scar seems to be about four or five years old, and I swear it was before he left... that's perjury, and I might go to jail. Well, your persuasion doesn't help now... no... keeping a promise is one thing... I know... I know you gave me money for this, but not much... well, okay, I'll listen to you, but I won't go... okay, okay, I'll keep it secret... what did you say?... how much?... that's a lot. How am I supposed to know you've already gotten it, even if... well, yes, of course, this will have an impact. Did you swear you had nothing to do with it? \u2014 I mean, killing... not, I believe you won't. Of course, I understand... sometimes mistakes happen because there are too many people... but it's not your fault... you always make things sound so convincing... you always do... well, okay, I'll think about it, but I have to act quickly... tomorrow? What time?... yes... yes, I'll come, but no check. Maybe it will be canceled... I really don't know how I got into this trouble... okay. Well, if you say so... well, I don't mean to be mean... so, okay.\" [2922] She leaves the post office, walking unsteadily along the sidewalk, smiling to herself. [2923] For the sake of that money, it's worth dealing with the police, taking a risk. It will make her life much better afterwards. Besides, it's not really much of a risk. She can say she forgot or didn't remember. Many women don't even remember what happened a year ago. She can say she mixed Harry with another man. Oh, she could come up with many excuses. Mrs. Rival is a naturally fickle person. Her mind is now as active as it was when she was incredibly depressed. She begins to seriously consider the few things she wants to do with the money...\n[2924] \u2014\n[2925] Mrs. Rival is a naturally fickle person. Her mind is now as active as it was when she was incredibly depressed. She begins to seriously consider the few things she wants to do with the money...\n[2926] Chapter 27\n[2927] Colin Lamb's Narrative\n[2928] 1\n[2929] \"You don't seem to have learned anything more from Mrs. Lyme.\u201d Colonel Baker complained. [2930] \"There wasn't much to learn.\" [2931] \"You're sure?\" [2932] \"Yes.\" [2933] \"She wasn't one of them?\" [2934] \"No.\" [2935] Colonel Beker gave me a meaningful look. \"Satisfied?\" he asked. [2936] \"Not really.\" [2937] \"Want to know more?\" [2938] \"The truth hasn't come out yet.\" [2939] \"Hmm, we should check out other places... Give up on New Moon Street. Right?\" [2940] \"Yes.\" \"You don't want to talk. Got drunk yesterday?\" [2941] \"I haven't done a good job.\" [2942] \"Want me to pat your head and say, 'There, there'?\" [2943] I couldn't help but chuckle. [2944] \"That's better,\" said Baker. \"Now, what's going on? Is it because of that girl?\" [2945] I shook my head. \"I've been feeling down for a while.\" [2946] \"I've noticed,\" Baker said unexpectedly. \"The world is in chaos now. Problems aren't as clear as they used to be. Once you feel defeated, it's like you've dried up. Be careful of those poisonous mushrooms growing over the wall! If they get the upper hand, your value to us ends. You've done some good work, kid. You should be satisfied with that and go back to your seaweed.\" [2947] He paused and said, \"Do you really 'like' those awful things, right?\" [2948] \"I find the topic interesting.\" [2949] \"I think it should be disgusting. It's an extraordinary mutation, isn't it? I mean, taste. How's the murder case you're handling going? I bet it's that girl who did it.\" [2950] \"You're wrong,\" I said. [2951] Beck waved his finger at me, with a stern yet affectionate expression. [2952] \"I told you to be prepared. I don't see this as a matter for the Boy Scouts.\" [2953] I walked down Charing Cross Road, lost in deep thought. [2954] I bought a newspaper at the subway station. [2955] I read that yesterday, during rush hour, a woman collapsed and lost consciousness at Victoria Station due to exhaustion and was sent to the hospital. Upon arrival at the hospital, it was discovered that she had been stabbed. She did not regain consciousness and died. [2956] Her name was Madame Melina Rival. [2957] 2\n[2958] I called Hakast. [2959] \"Yes,\" he answered my question. \"As the newspaper said.\" [2960] His voice sounded cold and unfeeling. [2961] \"I went to see her the other night. I told her that the scar hadn't been identified yet. The scar tissue had been recently produced through analysis. People make mistakes sometimes. It's just an attempt to do something they shouldn't. Someone paid this woman to confirm that the corpse was her long-abandoned husband. [2962] \"She did it! She thought that if she remembered the insignificant scar later, it would make it more convincing and help with the final confirmation. If she blurted it out immediately, it would sound too casual.\" [2963] \"So, Madame Melina Riviere got caught up in it?\" [2964] \"Do you know, I'm very doubtful about that. Suppose an old friend or acquaintance went to her and said, 'Listen, I'm in a bit of a pickle. A guy I did business with was murdered. If they confirm his identity, all our deals will be exposed, and it will be the end of us. But if you go there and say he's your husband, Harry Castleton, who left you years ago, the whole thing will blow over.'\" [2965] \"Wouldn't she hesitate and refuse to do it, saying it's too risky?\" [2966] \"If so, the person would say, 'What risk? At worst, you'll say you got it wrong. Any woman after fifteen years could possibly have forgotten.\u2019 It's likely that the person mentioned a substantial amount of money at this point. Then she agreed. She played it cool! She went ahead and did it.\" [2967] \"Without any doubt?\" [2968] \"She's not a suspicious woman. Why, oh God, Colin, every time we catch a killer, the people who know him can't believe he would do such a thing!\" [2969] \"What happened when you went to see her?\" [2970] \"I scared her. After I left, she did what I thought she would do - contact the man or woman who got her into this mess. Of course, I followed her. She went to the post office and made a call from a public phone booth. Unfortunately, it wasn't the phone booth I wanted her to use, the one at the end of the street. She had to go get change first. When she came out of the phone booth, she looked happy. She had been under our surveillance, and nothing suspicious had happened until last night. She went to Victoria Station, bought a ticket to Crofton. It was 6:30, rush hour traffic. She wasn't on her guard. She thought, no matter what, she had to see the person in Crofton. But the cunning devil was right in front of her. In the crowded crowd, wanting to gather behind someone and stab them\u2026 it was the easiest thing in the world. Don't imagine she knew she was being stabbed. People don't know, you understand. Remember Barton in the Laverty gang robbery? He walked down the street and fell dead only after a moment of pain. You think you'll be fine in a while. But you won't. You'll die standing up, only you won't know it beforehand.\"\n <Assistant> I have read the book and I will answer the question you ask.\n\nPassage 9:\nDialogue between User and Assistant 2026-06-12 03:44:54 \\n<User> The following context is the book I have read: \nThe exact time will be recorded at Somerset House. Where did you get married? Do you remember the location?\" he asked. [2263] \"In a place called Dunbrooke, St. Michael's Church, I think that's the name of the church. I really don't want to think that this happened over twenty years ago. It would make me feel like I have one foot in the grave,\" Mrs. Rival said. [2264] She stood up and extended her hand. Hakaste shook her hand and bid her farewell. He returned to the table, sat down, and tapped the table with his pencil. Soon after, Detective Inspector Gray entered. [2265] \"Satisfied?\" he asked. [2266] \"Seems so, detective,\" said Poirot. \"The name is Harry Castleton, which may be an alias. We'll see what we can find out about this man. It seems that more than one woman is out to get him for revenge.\" [2267] \"He looks quite respectable,\" said Grey. [2268] \"That's just his modus operandi,\" said Poirot. [2269] He began to ponder the clock with the inscription \"Rosemary.\" Was it a souvenir? [2270] Chapter XXII\n[2271] Narrated by Colin Lamb\n[2272] 1\n[2273] \"You're back,\" said Hercule Poirot. [2274] He carefully placed the bookmark in the book. This time, there was a cup of hot coffee on the table next to him. Poirot's taste in beverages was always a mystery! But this time, he didn't offer to share. [2275] \"How are you?\" I asked. [2276] \"Too noisy, too noisy. They're renovating, redecorating, and the whole structure of these apartments seems to have changed.\u201d\n[2277] \"Won't they improve it?\" [2278] \"I suppose so, but for me, it's the most irritating. I have to rearrange my schedule. There's even the smell of paint!\" [2279] He waved his hand, as if trying to brush away these troubles, and asked,\n[2280] \"How's your work going, then?\" [2281] I slowly said, \"I don't know.\" [2282] \"Oh, there may be such a situation.\" [2283] \"I found what they wanted me to find. But I didn't find that man. I don't know what they want. Intelligence? Or a corpse?\" [2284] \"Speaking of corpses, I read the statement from the delayed trial in Crocetta. It said someone was murdered intentionally. The name of the deceased has finally been found out.\" [2285] I nodded. [2286] \"Who is Harry Castleton?\" [2287] \"His wife identified him. Have you been to Crofton?\" [2288] \"Not yet. I'm planning to go tomorrow.\" [2289] \"Oh, do you have any free time?\" [2290] \"No. I'm still busy with work. My work keeps me \u2013\" I paused and continued, \"During my time abroad, I had no idea what was happening. Is it just someone claiming the body? What do you think about this?\" [2291] Poirot shrugged. [2292] \"It's still under investigation.\" [2293] \"Yes, the police have been doing a good job\u2014\"\n[2294] \"Mostly because the wife is very cooperative.\" [2295] \"Mrs. Melina Rival! A nice name!\" [2296] \"This reminds me of something,\" said Poirot. \"What could it be?\" [2297] He looked at me thoughtfully, but I couldn't help him. I knew Poirot, and the name probably reminded him of something. [2298] \"I visited a friend at a country house,\" Poirot mused, then shook his head. \"No, that was a long time ago.\" [2299] \"When I get back to London, I'll come and tell you everything I find out about Mrs. Melina Rival from Hercule,\" I promised him. [2300] Poirot waved his hand and said, \"That won't be necessary.\" [2301] \"You mean you already know everything about her?\" I asked. [2302] \"No, I mean I'm not interested in her\u2014\"\n[2303] \"You're not interested? I don't understand,\" I shook my head. [2304] \"Focus on the essential elements. Now, tell me about the girl named Ina\u2014the one who died in the phone booth on Wilbraham Crescent.\" [2305] \"I've told you everything I know about her. I have no more information on the girl.\" [2306] \"So, everything you know is, or can tell me is, that you saw the girl at the print shop looking like a pitiful little white rabbit, her heel stuck in the grille, and she looked angry\u2014\" He suddenly stopped talking. \"Where was that grille, by the way?\" [2307] \"How would I know, Polo?\" [2308] \"If you asked, you would know. How can you learn details if you don't ask more questions at the right time?\" [2309] \"But knowing where her heel fell off, what difference does that make?\" [2310] \"Perhaps it doesn't matter. In other words, we should know where the girl was at that time, which may be related to the people she saw or the events that happened there.\" [2311] \"You're just making things up. Anyway, I do know it was near the office. She said she bought a round loaf and then limped back to the office in her stockings. She ate the bread there and said she was wondering how she would get home.\" [2312] \"Oh, so how did she get home?\" Poirot asked with interest. [2313] \"I don't know.\" [2314] \"Oh, that's impossible. You'll never get to the bottom of things like this. That's why you only learn irrelevant information.\" [2315] \"You'd better go to Croisset yourself and ask those questions.\" I said angrily. [2316] \"I can't now. Next week there's an auction of a very interesting author's manuscript\u2014\"\n[2317] \"Still your obsession?\" [2318] \"Yes, indeed.\u201d His eyes sparkled. \"Buy John Dickson Carr's works, or as he sometimes called himself, Carter Dickson\u2014\"\n[2319] I left him before he could start, pretending to be rushing to an important appointment. I didn't have the patience to listen to him enumerate the past masters of detective novels. [2320] 2\n[2321] The next evening, I sat on the steps of Harkness's house, and when he came back, I felt as if I had suddenly come out of a depression. [2322] \"Hey, Colin? Is that you? You just popped up again, right?\" [2323] \"It would be more pleasant if you disappeared for good.\" [2324] \"How long have you been here, sitting on my house steps?\" [2325] \"Oh, about half an hour.\" [2326] \"I'm sorry I couldn't let you in.\" [2327] \"I could have easily gone in myself,\" I said angrily, \"but you don't know what training we've had!\" [2328] \"Why didn't you go in then?\" [2329] \"I didn't want to tarnish your reputation,\" I explained, \"if a detective's house was so easily broken into, it would be embarrassing for you.\" [2330] Hakast took out a key from his pocket and opened the door. [2331] \"Come in,\" he said, \"stop making excuses.\" [2332] He walked towards the living room to prepare some drinks. [2333] \"When did you get back?\" [2334] I said not long ago, and we both took our drinks and sat down. [2335] \"We've made some progress,\" Hakaste said, \"we've identified the victim.\" [2336] \"I saw the newspaper report. Who's Harry Castleton?\" [2337] \"A man who seemed respectable. He made a living by marrying or getting engaged to wealthy and gullible women who admired his financial expertise and entrusted their savings to him to manage. Then, he disappeared without a trace.\" [2338] \"He doesn't seem like that kind of person,\" I said, thinking back on it. [2339] \"That's exactly what made him successful.\" [2340] \"But hasn't he been charged with anything?\" [2341] \"No. We conducted an investigation, but it didn't go well. He often changes his name. Although they believe Harry Castleton, Raymond Blair, Lawrence Dalton, and Roger Byron are all the same person, they cannot prove it. Those women, you understand, won't say anything. They'd rather lose their money. This person's name changes constantly, suddenly appearing here or there, always profiting in the same way. It's too unbelievable. There were rumors that Roger Byron disappeared from the south, and a Lawrence Dalton was active near the River Tyne in Newcastle. He's shy about taking photos and always avoids having his girlfriend snap quick shots of him. So, these are events that happened a long time ago, around fifteen to twenty years ago. After that, he seemed to have truly disappeared. There were rumors that he had died, but some said he had gone abroad\u2014\"\n[2342] \"Regardless, there has been no news of him until he died on the carpet in Miss Pemaroo's living room?\" I said. [2343] \"Exactly.\" [2344] \"But there are many possible causes of his death.\" [2345] \"Indeed.\" [2346] \"Women never forget the insults they've suffered,\" I reminded him. [2347] \"You're right. Some women never forget\u2014\"\n[2348] \"If this woman later became blind, wouldn't that be adding insult to injury\u2014\"\n[2349] \"It's just speculation. There's nothing to confirm it.\" [2350] \"What did his wife look like? What was her name? Melina Rival? What an odd name! It couldn't be her name, could it?\" [2351] \"Her real name is Flora Gapp. She came up with it herself. It suited her at the time.\" [2352] \"What was she doing? A prostitute?\" [2353] \"Not exactly.\" [2354] \"What was she, past tense? A socialite, I believe.\" [2355] \"What I mean is that she was a good-natured woman, always there for her friends. She said she used to be an actress. Occasionally did some 'hostess' work. She was well-liked.\" [2356] \"Is all of that true?\" [2357] \"Quite credible. She was very certain when she identified the body. Not a moment's hesitation.\" [2358] \"That's lucky.\" [2359] \"Yes. At first, I was desperate. There were so many letters from wives! Then I began to think that a woman who could recognize her husband was a smart woman. Listen, I think Mrs. Rival knows more about her husband than she's letting on.\" [2360] \"Has she ever been involved in any criminal activities?\" [2361] \"No criminal record. I think she might have had some, maybe still has some, questionable friends. Nothing serious, just messing around, that sort of thing.\" [2362] \"What about the clocks?\" [2363] \"They don't mean anything to her. I think she's telling the truth. We traced the clocks' origins \u2013 the gilded clock and the Dresden porcelain clock were found at the Pombal Market [1]. It was no help at all! You should have seen the place on Saturday. The vendor thought an American girl bought them, but I think it's just a guess. There were so many American tourists at the Pombal Market. Her wife said a man bought them. But she couldn't remember what he looked like. The silver clock was bought from a silversmith in Bournemouth. A tall woman wanted to buy a birthday gift for her little daughter! She only remembers she was wearing a green hat.\" [2364] \"What about the fourth clock? The one that disappeared?\" [2365] \"That's none of your business.\" Huckleberry said. [2366] I knew what he meant. [2367] * * *\n[2368][1] Pimlico Market, located in the Notting Hill district, is one of the most famous outdoor markets in Britain, consisting of three different markets. The antique market at the southern end is the main destination for tourists to shop, and it is only open on Saturdays. [2369] Chapter 23\n[2370] Narrative by Colin Lamb\n[2371] I live in a small hotel near the police station. The barbecue is not bad, but that's about it. Otherwise, of course, it's very cheap. [2372] At ten o'clock in the morning, I called the Cavendish Typewriting Agency to say that I wanted a shorthand typist to type some letters and retype a commercial contract. My name is Douglas Weesby, and I live at the Clarendon Hotel (old hotels always have grand names). Is Miss Hilary Webber available? A friend of mine said she was excellent. [2373] I was lucky. Miss Hilary could come straight over. But she had an appointment at twelve. I said we could finish before then, as I had an appointment at twelve too. [2374] I waited at the Clarendon Hotel's revolving door, and Miss Hilary arrived. I went up to her. [2375] \"Mr. Douglas Weesby is at your service.\" I said. [2376] \"Did you make the call?\" [2377] \"Yes, I did.\" [2378] \"How could you do such a thing?\" She looks a bit angry. [2379] \"Why not? I am prepared to pay Cavendish Typewriting Agency. What difference does it make if we spend our precious time together at the Golden Daffodil Caf\u00e9 instead of dictating those boring letters? Let's go to a quiet place for a cup of coffee.\" [2380] The Golden Daffodil Caf\u00e9 truly lives up to its name, with its overall decor in bright and bold yellow. The Formica tabletop, plastic cushion, and cups and plates are all in a pale yellow. [2381] I ordered two coffees and scones. It was early, so the coffee shop was only the two of us. [2382] When the waitress took the order and left, we looked at each other across the table. [2383] \"How are you, Hilary?\" [2384] \"What do you mean? Am I okay?\" [2385] Dark circles under her eyes made her eyes appear lavender inst